BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:khoj-mozilla-counterstructures-residency-2026@artificialnouveau.github.
 io
DTSTAMP:20260905T080002Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260913
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260914
SUMMARY:Deadline: Khoj x Mozilla Foundation: Counterstructures Residency 20
 26 (Delhi)
DESCRIPTION:Counterstructures is Mozilla Foundation's cultural R&D residenc
 y\, in which creative technologists prototype alternatives to dominant tec
 h systems: cultural artifacts\, installations and tools that question and 
 reframe them. Each edition operationalises the findings of a regional part
 icipatory assembly into thematic tracks\; the Delhi edition\, hosted by Kh
 oj International Artists' Association\, works from Mozilla's assemblies wi
 th G5A in Delhi and Mumbai and runs on two tracks. RETAINING CULTURAL AUTH
 ORITY asks what happens when oral\, embodied or community-held knowledge i
 s converted into datasets and machine-readable forms\, which cultural mate
 rials become visible to AI systems and which do not\, and how communities 
 can retain authority over what is digitised\, interpreted\, reused or deli
 berately kept private. REMEMBERING AND THINKING WITH MACHINES asks where t
 hought resides when people think with machines\, at what stage human judgm
 ent enters the system\, and what cultural or professional knowledge is los
 t when judgment is standardised through automation. Proposals may respond 
 to one or both tracks and can take many forms\, but must critically counte
 r the questions the tracks raise rather than simply illustrate them. WHAT 
 RESIDENTS DO: prototype an artifact\, installation\, system\, artwork or t
 ool for public exhibition\; take part in listening\, reading\, workshops a
 nd facilitated critique\, testing ideas with peers and community members\;
  write a 750-word report on the project's development and insights for the
  Counterstructures report and online archive\; and present the work public
 ly. WHO IT IS FOR: creative technologists and multidisciplinary artists us
 ing technology as a medium for cultural inquiry\, bringing critical perspe
 ctives and research-driven practice. Experience with AI can be early-stage
  or advanced\, but SOME PROFICIENCY WITH CODING IS REQUIRED. ELIGIBILITY: 
 this Delhi edition is open to Indian nationals only. Earlier editions ran 
 with tiat in San Francisco (responding to Hollywood's 8 Rules for AI) and 
 with Rhizome in New York\, whose residents showed at the New Museum. APPLY
  through the Mozilla Foundation form at https://mozillafoundation.tfaforms
 .net/f/khojresidency\; the guiding principles for the call are set out in 
 the form. Deadline 13 September 2026.\n\nOrganisation: Khoj International 
 Artists' Association in collaboration with Mozilla Foundation\nLocation: N
 ew Delhi\, India. Weekly in-person sessions at Khoj. INDIAN NATIONALS ONLY
 .\nAward: Mozilla's Creative Futures: Counterstructures programme publishe
 s USD 2\,500 per resident (USD 2\,000 stipend plus a USD 500 materials bud
 get)\, along with weekly in-person work sessions\, guest lectures from pra
 ctitioners across art\, technology and community design\, and publication 
 of the finished project in the collective documentation report. CAVEAT: ne
 ither the Khoj call page nor the Mozilla application form restates a figur
 e for this six-week Delhi edition\, and the published package covers the t
 en-week format\, so confirm the amount with Khoj before applying. No appli
 cation fee.\nDuration: Six weeks\, 1 October to 12 November 2026\, running
  weekly in Delhi\, with final projects presented publicly 13 to 15 Novembe
 r 2026.\n\nMore: https://khojstudios.org/opportunity/open-call-counterstru
 ctures-residency/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referra
 l
URL:https://khojstudios.org/opportunity/open-call-counterstructures-residen
 cy/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:New Delhi\, India. Weekly in-person sessions at Khoj. INDIAN NATIO
 NALS ONLY.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ctm-resynthesising-traditional-at-the-extreme-2027@artificialnouveau.gi
 thub.io
DTSTAMP:20260905T080002Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260928
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260929
SUMMARY:Deadline: CTM Festival: Resynthesising the Traditional 2027 Artisti
 c Lab (At the Extreme)
DESCRIPTION:Resynthesising the Traditional is CTM Festival's long-term init
 iative co-curated by Stas Sharifulla\, taking resynthesis (the computer-mu
 sic method of analysing a sound to extract its components) as a metaphor f
 or critically engaging with tradition and heritage\, against conservative 
 views of culture as frozen and untouchable. The artistic lab at its heart 
 is an open toolbox for artists connecting to the traditional outside exoti
 cisation\, cultural essentialism and uneven power dynamics. THEME\, AT THE
  EXTREME: this edition invites artists and practice-based researchers whos
 e work examines the ethical\, aesthetic and political implications of trad
 itions entangled with extremity: intensity\, transgression\, transformatio
 n\, marginality\, outsiderness\, endurance\, conflict and excess. The call
  explicitly looks past the usual noise\, violence and loud-popular-music t
 ropes and past masculine codings of the extreme\, toward current queer and
  feminist reframings\, treating extremity as a relational condition\, chos
 en or imposed\, produced between majoritarian centres and their minoritari
 an outsides. Guiding questions include what it means to follow a tradition
  to its extreme consequences\, who is allowed to push tradition to its lim
 its and whose practices get framed as inauthentic or dangerous\, what it m
 eans to practise tradition under war\, displacement\, repression or ecolog
 ical crisis\, and how traditions might offer ways of inhabiting extreme ti
 mes. Preference goes to work engaging technological\, bodily\, aesthetic a
 nd theoretical forms at that intersection\, especially in unexpected or ra
 rely recognised ways. WHAT HAPPENS: theory and practice workshops from the
  co-hosts with individual and group exercises\, closed 15 to 20 minute ora
 l presentations by each participant\, a solo public performance or present
 ation (15 to 20 minutes) at a free-entry event\, inputs from specialists s
 uch as artists\, researchers and technologists\, guided individual and gro
 up sonic improvisations\, field trips and site visits\, and feedback sessi
 ons with the co-hosts and curators. AFTERWARDS: participants may propose n
 ew artistic work or research\, solo or in collaborations formed at the lab
 \, to the artistic team\; selected works are shortlisted\, and up to two a
 re commissioned and presented at CTM in January/February 2028. ELIGIBILITY
 : an existing practice tied to (re)connecting with traditional ideas and s
 ounds through a contemporary lens and how that overlaps with the extreme\,
  plus clear ideas or questions about the next step you want to take. Appro
 aches may be technological\, instrument-focused\, compositional\, aestheti
 c\, research-oriented or otherwise. Working knowledge of English is recomm
 ended\, and any concerns about that can be flagged in the form. Up to six 
 participants are selected by co-hosts Karina Utomo (extreme metal vocalist
 \, composer and collaborative artist) and Luigi Monteanni (sound researche
 r and ethnomusicologist completing a PhD at SOAS on extreme metal in Bandu
 ng). The lab is a time-intensive full-day commitment and participants must
  agree to give the short public performance. APPLY through the form with a
  portfolio link (PDF\, max 10 pages\, public access\, works from the last 
 five years\, text kept minimal) and a link to a specific audio track or se
 ction (max five minutes) that best represents your current practice. CTM a
 sks that AI support rather than replace your own voice in the application.
  TIMELINE: deadline 28 September 2026\; participants announced mid to late
  October 2026. Questions to resynth@ctm-festival.de.\n\nOrganisation: CTM 
 Festival\, co-curated by Stas Sharifulla\nLocation: Berlin\, Germany. Open
  to artists worldwide\; solo artists and projects only.\nAward: Travel bud
 get of up to EUR 300 per person\, plus accommodation and catering for the 
 duration of the lab and a festival pass to CTM 2027 events. Visa fees are 
 supported where required\, and letters of invitation are provided for visa
  or funding applications. NO artist fee or stipend for the lab itself. Aft
 erwards\, up to two new works arising from the lab may be commissioned and
  presented at CTM Festival in January/February 2028. No application fee.\n
 Duration: Seven days: arrival 24 January\, lab 26 to 31 January 2027 (10:0
 0 to 19:00 daily)\, public presentations 26 January\, departure 1 February
  2027.\n\nMore: https://www.ctm-festival.de/festival-2027/open-calls/resyn
 thesising-the-traditional-2027?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_m
 edium=referral
URL:https://www.ctm-festival.de/festival-2027/open-calls/resynthesising-the
 -traditional-2027?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referra
 l
LOCATION:Berlin\, Germany. Open to artists worldwide\; solo artists and pro
 jects only.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:sat-research-creation-residency-arts-technologies-2026-28@artificialnou
 veau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260905T080002Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261018
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261019
SUMMARY:Deadline: SAT: Research-Creation Residency Program in Arts and Tech
 nologies 2026-28 (Montreal)
DESCRIPTION:The Societe des arts technologiques (SAT) in Montreal runs a re
 search-creation residency program in arts and technologies for artists and
  collectives working in the performing arts and/or media arts. The residen
 cy funds a research-creation stage of a project in technological arts rath
 er than the finalisation of a finished work\, so the call weighs the explo
 ration process and the innovation in it above a polished outcome. Each sel
 ected project receives a CAD 10\,000 grant\, whether the applicant is an i
 ndividual artist or a collective\, plus access to SAT spaces and equipment
 \, technical and creative support from the SAT teams\, mentoring and train
 ing activities\, and documentation and archiving. Access to spaces and equ
 ipment is assessed against the project's needs and granted subject to avai
 lability. Travel\, meal and accommodation costs beyond the grant are not c
 overed. Open to professional artists and artistic collectives worldwide\, 
 at any level of experience and from any primary discipline\, provided the 
 profile and project suit immersive technologies\; collectives must submit 
 a joint portfolio showing they can carry out the project independently\, p
 articularly the visual and sound creation inside a dome. Each application 
 must include a form and supporting documents setting out the concept\, the
  technical solutions considered\, and what will be explored during the res
 idency. One application per project and per person\; incomplete applicatio
 ns are not evaluated\; use permanent\, downloadable links rather than WeTr
 ansfer or similar temporary services. Accommodations and alternative suppo
 rt documents (large print and so on) are available on request at residence
 s@sat.qc.ca. Deadline 18 October 2026. Apply through the Google Form linke
 d from the SAT open-call page.\n\nOrganisation: Societe des arts technolog
 iques (SAT)\nLocation: Societe des arts technologiques\, 1201 Boulevard Sa
 int-Laurent\, Montreal\, QC\, Canada. Open to professional artists and col
 lectives from anywhere in the world\; no nationality restriction stated.\n
 Award: CAD 10\,000 grant per selected project\, for individual artists and
  collectives alike. Also included: access to SAT workspaces during the res
 idency periods\, access to equipment and software resources subject to ava
 ilability\, mentoring and training activities (on site and remote)\, creat
 ive and technical support from the SAT teams\, and documentation and archi
 ving of the work. Travel\, meals and accommodation beyond the grant are NO
 T covered. No application fee.\nDuration: Two months\, extendable up to th
 ree months depending on the project\; residencies run across the 2026-28 p
 rogram.\n\nMore: https://sat.qc.ca/en/calls/research-creation-residency-pr
 ogram-in-arts-technologies-2026-28?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&u
 tm_medium=referral
URL:https://sat.qc.ca/en/calls/research-creation-residency-program-in-arts-
 technologies-2026-28?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=refe
 rral
LOCATION:Societe des arts technologiques\, 1201 Boulevard Saint-Laurent\, M
 ontreal\, QC\, Canada. Open to professional artists and collectives from a
 nywhere in the world\; no nationality restriction stated.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:uj-artists-in-residence-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260905T080002Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260831
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260901
SUMMARY:Deadline: University of Johannesburg Artists in Residence 2027
DESCRIPTION:UJ Arts & Culture's annual residency gives artists a monthly st
 ipend and an institutional base at the University of Johannesburg for ten 
 months\, with the option of a remote rather than physical residency. Disci
 plines covered are visual and fine arts\, music\, theatre\, performance an
 d dance\, design\, film and television\, and literary arts. A virtual info
 rmation session with Pieter Jacobs\, Head of UJ Arts & Culture\, was sched
 uled for 17 August 2026 to walk applicants through the process. ELIGIBILIT
 Y: applicants must hold a relevant post-school qualification\, with prefer
 ence for master's and PhD holders\, and must not be affiliated with anothe
 r tertiary institution in South Africa. Preference has historically gone t
 o artists with African connections. APPLICATION: via the online form linke
 d from the UJ Arts & Culture residency page\; contact kmachoga@uj.ac.za or
  +27 11 559 1476 with questions. DEADLINE: 31 August 2026. No fee.\n\nOrga
 nisation: UJ Arts & Culture\, University of Johannesburg\nLocation: Johann
 esburg\, South Africa. Both physical and remote residencies can be applied
  for\, though office\, studio\, rehearsal and performance space is limited
  and not guaranteed.\nAward: Stipend of ZAR 12\,000 per month across a 10-
 month residency\, so roughly ZAR 120\,000 in total. No travel or accommoda
 tion budget on top of the stipend\, and studio or rehearsal space is limit
 ed rather than guaranteed. No application fee.\nDuration: 10 months during
  2027\n\nMore: https://arts.uj.ac.za/artists-in-residence/?utm_source=arti
 ficialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://arts.uj.ac.za/artists-in-residence/?utm_source=artificialnouvea
 ugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Johannesburg\, South Africa. Both physical and remote residencies 
 can be applied for\, though office\, studio\, rehearsal and performance sp
 ace is limited and not guaranteed.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:delfina-foundation-curators-central-west-africa-2027@artificialnouveau.
 github.io
DTSTAMP:20260905T080002Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260830
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260831
SUMMARY:Deadline: Delfina Foundation: Open Call for Curators Based in Centr
 al and West Africa (London residency)
DESCRIPTION:With support from Delfina Foundation's Network of Patrons for A
 frica\, Delfina is offering a six- or twelve-week London residency within 
 its winter 2027 season to curators based in Central and West Africa. ELIGI
 BILITY: applicants must hold nationality and be based in one of Angola\, B
 enin\, Burkina Faso\, Burundi\, Cabo Verde\, Cameroon\, Central African Re
 public\, Chad\, Cote d'Ivoire\, Democratic Republic of the Congo\, Equator
 ial Guinea\, Gabon\, Ghana\, Guinea\, Guinea-Bissau\, Liberia\, Mali\, Mau
 ritania\, Niger\, Nigeria\, Republic of the Congo\, Sao Tome and Principe\
 , Senegal\, Sierra Leone\, The Gambia or Togo\; and must have at least fiv
 e years of professional curatorial practice in a freelance\, academic or i
 nstitutional context. THE PROGRAMME: alongside time and space to develop t
 heir work\, residents get guided visits to museums\, galleries\, non-profi
 ts\, artist-run spaces and studios\, plus trips to institutions outside Lo
 ndon\; opportunities to meet and engage with artists\, curators\, academic
 s and researchers through organised presentations\, studio visits\, events
  and meals\; professional development including presentations and crits\, 
 portfolio reviews and mentoring activities to strengthen their practice\; 
 and collaborative exchange between residents through informal gatherings a
 nd meals. APPLY via the application form on the open call page\; Delfina s
 trongly recommends reading the Guidance Notes document carefully before st
 arting. Deadline Sunday 30 August 2026\, 23:59 BST.\n\nOrganisation: Delfi
 na Foundation\nLocation: Delfina Foundation\, 29-31 Catherine Place\, Lond
 on\, UK. Applicants must hold nationality and be based in an eligible Cent
 ral or West African country.\nAward: Fully supported: a return economy-cla
 ss flight\, airport transfers in the UK\, reimbursement of visa fees if re
 quired\, a living allowance of GBP 40 per day\, a materials and research a
 llowance of GBP 70 per week\, a travel pass for London's public transport 
 network\, and a single bedroom with a desk at Delfina Foundation\, a house
  hosting up to eight residents with shared bathrooms\, laundry rooms\, kit
 chen\, library and dining room. Supported by Delfina Foundation's Network 
 of Patrons for Africa. No application fee.\nDuration: Six or twelve weeks 
 within Delfina's winter 2027 season\, which runs 5 January to 28 March 202
 7.\n\nMore: https://www.delfinafoundation.com/open-calls/current/open-call
 -for-curators-based-in-central-and-west-africa/?utm_source=artificialnouve
 augrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.delfinafoundation.com/open-calls/current/open-call-for-cura
 tors-based-in-central-and-west-africa/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantde
 sk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Delfina Foundation\, 29-31 Catherine Place\, London\, UK. Applican
 ts must hold nationality and be based in an eligible Central or West Afric
 an country.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:surels-place-residency-2027-winter@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260905T080002Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260901
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260902
SUMMARY:Deadline: Surel's Place: Month-Long Artist Residency (September 202
 6 deadline)
DESCRIPTION:Surel's Place is a residency in Garden City\, Idaho with a two-
 fold mission: to give artists the time\, shelter and professional support 
 their work deserves\, and to give the public accessible art experiences in
 cluding performances\, readings\, exhibits and workshops. In exchange for 
 the residency\, artists offer art experiences back to the community. OPEN 
 TO professional visual\, literary and performance artists: painters\, writ
 ers\, musicians\, architects\, filmmakers\, choreographers\, any artist wh
 o needs a place to focus. LIMITS: because of the property's constraints an
 d proximity to neighbours\, it cannot accommodate loud or very messy proce
 sses such as welding or blacksmithing. The organisers also note that predo
 minantly conceptual work\, work that must be explained to attract a viewer
 \, may not be met with as much enthusiasm by the jurors\, and that they we
 lcome artists who want to connect with an audience. Each cycle\, one of th
 e five residencies includes outreach to local schools through the PARTNER 
 Schools Program. REQUIREMENTS: maintain residence at the home and studio (
 no studio-use-only residencies)\; use the residency in a focused way towar
 ds a stated goal with no other employment or work happening simultaneously
 \; visual artists give a show of and/or lecture about their residency work
 \, writers give a reading and discussion session\; all artists offer at le
 ast one public workshop (2 to 3 hours\, minimal lecturing)\; visual artist
 s donate one piece of art to Surel's Place for future fundraising\; writer
 s allow Surel's Place to publish a free-standing excerpt of their residenc
 y work in books\, broadsides\, posters or similar. SALES COMMISSION applie
 s as set out in the funding notes. HOUSE RULES: the non-smoking requiremen
 t is non-negotiable and any evidence of smoking is grounds for early termi
 nation. Partners are welcome but children and pets cannot be accommodated\
 , no more than two adults may live there during a residency\, and partners
  wanting concurrent residencies must apply and be accepted separately. ELI
 GIBILITY: applicants must be 25 or older and non-smokers. APPLY by email t
 o info@surelsplace.org with the online Artist in Residence application plu
 s\, for VISUAL ARTISTS\, a cover letter introducing yourself and saying wh
 y you want to be at Surel's Place specifically\, a proposal including a wo
 rkshop outline\, a CV\, electronic images of 4 to 6 pieces of work and an 
 image list (title\, size\, medium\, year)\; for LITERARY applicants\, cove
 r letter\, proposal with workshop outline\, CV and PDF work samples with y
 our name and page number in the top right corner (fiction\, prose or scrip
 t no more than 10 typed double-spaced pages\; poetry no more than six poem
 s and no more than 10 pages)\; for OTHER MEDIUMS\, cover letter\, proposal
  with workshop outline\, CV and electronic samples (contact Surel's Place 
 to discuss sample requirements for your medium). Applications are reviewed
  by juries of accomplished artists\, writers and fine arts professionals\,
  and all applicants are notified by email.\n\nOrganisation: Surel's Place\
 nLocation: In person in Garden City\, Idaho\, USA (Boise area). Residents 
 must live at the home and studio\; no studio-only residencies.\nAward: Fre
 e rent\, utilities and wi-fi\, a living stipend of USD 100 per week\, a US
 D 300 travel stipend\, use of well-maintained bicycles\, free or discounte
 d tickets to art events and movies\, marketing through Surel's Place chann
 els\, and event hosting including logistics and installation help. Each re
 sident also gets a local host to orient them and host their public events.
  No application fee. NOTE: Surel's Place takes a commission on sales arisi
 ng from the residency\, 35% of retail price on artwork\, 45% on ticketed e
 vents that are offsite or need contracted services (calculated after admin
 istrative expenses)\, and 15% on books\, CDs or other merchandise tangenti
 al to the artist's medium. A surcharge may apply to cover additional utili
 ty costs if a partner joins.\nDuration: One month (longer may be possible 
 if justified in the application). The 1 September deadline covers residenc
 ies in January to May of the following year\, beginning four months after 
 the deadline\; the 1 March deadline covers July to November of the same ye
 ar.\n\nMore: https://surelsplace.org/month-long-residencies/?utm_source=ar
 tificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://surelsplace.org/month-long-residencies/?utm_source=artificialno
 uveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:In person in Garden City\, Idaho\, USA (Boise area). Residents mus
 t live at the home and studio\; no studio-only residencies.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:video-pool-matr-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260905T080002Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260830
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260831
SUMMARY:Deadline: Video Pool: Media Art + Technology Residency (MATR) 2027
DESCRIPTION:Video Pool's Media Art + Technology Residency supports experime
 ntal\, independent\, technology-based work. The selected artist or artists
  develop a new project (or adapt and transform an existing one) in direct 
 response to the intimate scale and specific character of the Poolside Gall
 ery\, culminating in an exhibition there. The call is deliberately open on
  medium: video\, sound\, installation\, digital media\, performance\, inte
 ractive technologies\, expanded cinema and interdisciplinary practices are
  all welcome. VP asks proposals to speak clearly to your practice\, the qu
 estions\, materials or ideas that drive the work\, and how that work might
  grow\, shift or take new form inside the gallery. It is explicitly intere
 sted in expansive proposals\, work that pushes past the gallery's four wal
 ls to engage its surrounding spaces (hallways\, windows\, projection wall)
  or that reimagines what the gallery itself can hold\, and in projects tha
 t engage technology as a creative\, critical\, aesthetic or socio-politica
 l tool. Priority is given to artists from equity-seeking communities whose
  practices contribute to the advancement of experimental media arts. Resid
 ents get full access to VP's available equipment\, facilities and technica
 l resources throughout the residency\, and applicants are encouraged to re
 view the equipment and facilities list before proposing. ELIGIBILITY: open
  to VP members (this is a member submission call)\; no nationality restric
 tion is stated\, but the residency and exhibition are in person in Winnipe
 g. APPLICATION: artist bio (1\,500 characters)\, headshot\, description of
  your artistic practice (3\,000 characters)\, project proposal for MATR (3
 \,000 characters)\, equipment and facility needs (750 characters)\, descri
 ption of how you would use the space (750 characters)\, CV\, up to five im
 ages (optional) and up to two video or documentation links. Submit via the
  online form at videopool.org/matr-2027-application/ by 30 August 2026\; a
 lternative submission formats can be arranged by contacting Emma (emma@vid
 eopool.org) or Iyunade (iyunade@videopool.org). General enquiries: info@vi
 deopool.org\, 204-949-9134.\n\nOrganisation: Video Pool Media Arts Centre\
 nLocation: In person at Video Pool Media Arts Centre and its Poolside Gall
 ery\, 221-100 Arthur Street\, Winnipeg\, Manitoba R3B 1H3\, Canada.\nAward
 : CAD 2\,250 artist fee\, covering both the residency period and the subse
 quent Poolside Gallery exhibition. Residents also get full access to VP's 
 equipment\, facilities and technical resources\; the Poolside Gallery itse
 lf is equipped with two projectors and four speakers. Applicants must be V
 P members\, so a membership is purchased separately\, but there is no sepa
 rate application fee.\nDuration: Up to two months to develop and install t
 he project\, including one month of dedicated access to the Poolside Galle
 ry. Residency takes place in Winter 2027\; exhibition dates TBD.\n\nMore: 
 https://videopool.org/media-arts-technology-residency-2027-call-for-applic
 ations/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://videopool.org/media-arts-technology-residency-2027-call-for-app
 lications/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:In person at Video Pool Media Arts Centre and its Poolside Gallery
 \, 221-100 Arthur Street\, Winnipeg\, Manitoba R3B 1H3\, Canada.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:stimuleringsfonds-videotage-residency-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260905T080002Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261021
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261022
SUMMARY:Deadline: Stimuleringsfonds: Open Call Residency Videotage 2027 (Ho
 ng Kong)
DESCRIPTION:The Creative Industries Fund NL (Stimuleringsfonds) runs this r
 esidency with Videotage\, a Hong Kong-based non-profit media arts institut
 ion specialising in the promotion\, presentation\, creation and preservati
 on of moving image and media art in all forms and languages. The Fund is r
 ecruiting one Netherlands-based designer\, maker or observer working in di
 gital culture to spend two or three months at Videotage's studio space in 
 Hong Kong in 2027\, conducting research\, broadening their network\, devel
 oping new work inspired by Hong Kong\, and presenting their findings or co
 mpleted works. The residency encourages crossovers and experimentation\, g
 iving the participant space to explore new methodologies\, widen their cre
 ative practice and produce work that pushes the boundaries of digital cult
 ure\, with a focus on social and urban dynamics. The 2027 edition takes th
 e technological 'black box' phenomenon as its starting point and invites t
 he resident to respond to that theme within the context of Videotage. ELIG
 IBILITY: you are an individual designer\, maker or observer within the fie
 ld of digital culture\; you are established in the Netherlands\, Aruba\, B
 onaire\, Curacao\, Saba\, Sint Eustatius or Sint Maarten (Kingdom of the N
 etherlands)\; you are registered in the business register of the Dutch Cha
 mber of Commerce (KvK) or another business register within the Kingdom of 
 the Netherlands\; and you have had a professional practice in the creative
  industry for at least four years. NOT open to duos\, collectives or coope
 rative ventures\, nor to designers and makers who receive a grant during t
 he work period under the Talent Development Grant Scheme\, the Open Call S
 elf-taught Designer or the Open Call Emerging Design Practices ABCSSS. Not
 e that accepting this open call means you cannot apply to those schemes an
 d open calls in 2027. FUNDING: EUR 5\,000 for two months or EUR 6\,500 for
  three months\, covering travel\, insurance\, living expenses\, compensati
 on for time invested and material and transport costs\; accommodation\, st
 udio use and Videotage's facilitation are covered separately by the Fund. 
 Submit your proposal to the Creative Industries Fund NL by 21 October 2026
 \, 16:00 CEST.\n\nOrganisation: Stimuleringsfonds Creatieve Industrie (Cre
 ative Industries Fund NL) in collaboration with Videotage\, Hong Kong\nLoc
 ation: Residency takes place at Videotage in Hong Kong. Applicants must be
  established in the Netherlands\, Aruba\, Bonaire\, Curacao\, Saba\, Sint 
 Eustatius or Sint Maarten (Kingdom of the Netherlands) and registered in a
  business register within the Kingdom of the Netherlands.\nAward: EUR 5\,0
 00 for a two-month residency or EUR 6\,500 for a three-month residency\, p
 aid by the Creative Industries Fund NL to cover travel\, insurance\, livin
 g expenses\, compensation for time invested\, and material and transport c
 osts. Accommodation\, use of the studio space and facilitation by Videotag
 e are covered directly by the Fund on top of this. No application fee.\nDu
 ration: Two or three months in 2027 (spring 2027)\; state your preferred p
 eriod in the application.\n\nMore: https://www.stimuleringsfonds.nl/en/gra
 nts/open-call-videotage?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=r
 eferral
URL:https://www.stimuleringsfonds.nl/en/grants/open-call-videotage?utm_sour
 ce=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Residency takes place at Videotage in Hong Kong. Applicants must b
 e established in the Netherlands\, Aruba\, Bonaire\, Curacao\, Saba\, Sint
  Eustatius or Sint Maarten (Kingdom of the Netherlands) and registered in 
 a business register within the Kingdom of the Netherlands.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ars-biologica-artxscience-residency-2027-2028@artificialnouveau.github.
 io
DTSTAMP:20260905T080002Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260928
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260929
SUMMARY:Deadline: Ars Biologica ArtXScience Residency 2027-2028 (Ars Electr
 onica x Ceske Budejovice ECoC 2028)
DESCRIPTION:A fully funded art-science residency from Ars Electronica and C
 eske Budejovice\, European Capital of Culture 2028\, exploring how art and
  science can collaborate on urgent environmental challenges: climate emerg
 ence\, sustainable agriculture\, geoengineering\, permaculture\, water res
 ource management\, and the broader ecological impacts on local communities
 . Selected residents work in direct collaboration with local scientists in
  Ceske Budejovice. WHO CAN APPLY: artists\, designers\, creatives and arti
 st collectives with more than 5 years of professional creative practice an
 d documented interdisciplinary work\; experience in community engagement a
 nd science collaborations is essential\; applicants must be 18+\, of any n
 ationality\, and legally eligible to work and travel in the EU. WHAT YOU G
 ET: a EUR 24\,000 package (EUR 6\,000 artist fee\, up to EUR 15\,000 produ
 ction\, up to EUR 3\,000 travel and subsistence) plus one month of housing
  covered separately\; two winners are selected. STRUCTURE: the residency r
 uns January 2027 to April 2028\, with a required on-site stay of up to one
  month in February/March 2027\, an optional stay in August/September 2027\
 , and a March 2028 stay for festival installation\, exhibition and deinsta
 llation. HOW TO APPLY: online form with project title\, a written proposal
  of max 250 words\, a scientific direction statement (max 100 words)\, art
 istic vision (max 100 words)\, community connection plan (max 100 words)\,
  a super short pitch (3 to 30 words)\, 3 to 7 images\, an optional 1-minut
 e video\, a portfolio link and contact information. Two-round selection (o
 nline application then interview)\; winners notified by November 2026. Dea
 dline 28 September 2026 at midnight CET.\n\nOrganisation: Ars Electronica 
 and Ceske Budejovice - European Capital of Culture 2028\nLocation: Ceske B
 udejovice\, Czech Republic. Open to applicants of any nationality aged 18+
  who are legally eligible to work and travel in the EU. Required on-site s
 tays: up to one month in February/March 2027\, an optional stay in August/
 September 2027\, and March 2028 for installation\, exhibition and deinstal
 lation.\nAward: EUR 24\,000 total package per winner: EUR 6\,000 artist fe
 e\, up to EUR 15\,000 production budget\, and up to EUR 3\,000 for travel\
 , accommodation and subsistence\, plus a separate budget covering one mont
 h of housing in Ceske Budejovice. Two positions available. Free to apply.\
 nDuration: January 2027 to April 2028\, with on-site blocks in early 2027 
 and March 2028\n\nMore: https://ars.electronica.art/export/en/ecoc2028-ars
 -biologica-art-science-residency/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&ut
 m_medium=referral
URL:https://ars.electronica.art/export/en/ecoc2028-ars-biologica-art-scienc
 e-residency/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Ceske Budejovice\, Czech Republic. Open to applicants of any natio
 nality aged 18+ who are legally eligible to work and travel in the EU. Req
 uired on-site stays: up to one month in February/March 2027\, an optional 
 stay in August/September 2027\, and March 2028 for installation\, exhibiti
 on and deinstallation.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:cpr-artist-in-residence-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260905T080002Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260824
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260825
SUMMARY:Deadline: CPR Center for Performance Research: Artist-in-Residence 
 Program 2027
DESCRIPTION:CPR's year-long Artist-in-Residence programme supports six New 
 York City-based artists working across contemporary dance\, performance an
 d time-based art. The organisation values experimental approaches to conte
 nt\, form and aesthetics and explicitly encourages risk-taking\, interdisc
 iplinarity and the unexpected\, so process-led and hard-to-categorise work
  is in scope rather than a liability. WHAT RESIDENTS RECEIVE: a $2\,000 st
 ipend\; 20 free hours of rehearsal space plus up to 150 further subsidised
  hours in CPR's two studios\, with priority and advance calendar access\; 
 participation in OPEN LAB (facilitated embodied workshops and feedback sho
 wings) and OPEN AiR (solo or shared presentations of current work)\; and a
 ccess to technical equipment and production support. ELIGIBILITY: artists 
 must be based in New York City and remain so throughout the cycle (no visa
  sponsorship)\; must have had work publicly presented before\; must not ha
 ve held a CPR residency in the last five years\; and current undergraduate
 s are not eligible. Individuals\, companies\, collaboratives and collectiv
 es may all apply\, each counting as a single residency. CPR strongly encou
 rages applications from BIPOC\, LGBTQ+\, immigrant and disabled artists an
 d from artists across generations. APPLICATION: a Google Form with narrati
 ve answers on the artist's central research focus and its alignment with C
 PR's values\; drafting answers in a separate document first is recommended
 . Selection is by an independent panel of artists\, curators and arts lead
 ers. Deadline: Monday 24 August 2026 at 5pm ET\; late submissions are not 
 accepted. Notifications November 2026. Questions: applications@cprnyc.org.
 \n\nOrganisation: CPR - Center for Performance Research\nLocation: Brookly
 n\, New York City\, USA. RESTRICTED: NYC-based artists only\; CPR does not
  sponsor visas\, and residents must remain NYC-based for the whole cycle.\
 nAward: $2\,000 stipend\, 20 free hours of rehearsal space\, up to 150 sub
 sidised rehearsal hours across CPR's two studios with priority and advance
  booking\, presentation slots in the OPEN LAB and OPEN AiR programmes\, an
 d access to technical equipment and production support. No application fee
 .\nDuration: One year\, January to December 2027\n\nMore: https://www.cprn
 yc.org/events/opencall-2027airprogram?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdes
 k&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.cprnyc.org/events/opencall-2027airprogram?utm_source=artifi
 cialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Brooklyn\, New York City\, USA. RESTRICTED: NYC-based artists only
 \; CPR does not sponsor visas\, and residents must remain NYC-based for th
 e whole cycle.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:surface-design-association-air-2026-27@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260905T080002Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260814
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260815
SUMMARY:Deadline: Surface Design Association: Artist in Residence 2026-27 (
 virtual)
DESCRIPTION:The Surface Design Association's Artist in Residence programme 
 is built to develop skills\, open up relationships and support fiber and t
 extile artists through a studio project that pushes their practice. The re
 sidency is entirely virtual and runs across a full year\, which is the poi
 nt: it removes the travel\, housing and time-away barriers that keep many 
 practitioners out of in-person residencies. One artist is selected annuall
 y by a panel of SDA staff\, board members and committee volunteers. WHAT I
 T INVOLVES: studio visits across the year\, social media programming\, and
  meetings with the SDA community\, plus further programming shaped around 
 the resident's own skills and interests\, which can include teaching onlin
 e workshops\, contributing to SDA publications\, jurying exhibitions or jo
 ining panels. FUNDING: a $2\,000 studio stipend for the project\, plus up 
 to $2\,000 in additional compensation for programming work\, targeting abo
 ut $4\,000 total. ELIGIBILITY: emerging\, mid-career and established artis
 ts\; US-based and international applicants welcome\; students and professi
 onals may apply\; applicants must hold current SDA membership (from $85 pe
 r year\, $60 for students). APPLICATION: a portfolio of 10 images with ful
 l artwork information (title\, year\, materials\, technique\, dimensions\,
  photo credit)\, a detailed CV\, essay responses on artistic goals\, progr
 amming interests and professional development\, and demographic informatio
 n. No application fee. The 2026-27 cycle closes 14 August 2026.\n\nOrganis
 ation: Surface Design Association (SDA)\nLocation: Fully virtual. Open to 
 US-based and international applicants\; current SDA membership is required
 .\nAward: $2\,000 studio stipend\, plus up to $2\,000 further compensation
  for programming work (SDA aims for roughly $4\,000 in total per resident)
 . No application fee\, but applicants must be current SDA members (members
 hip from $85 per year\, $60 for students).\nDuration: One year\, virtual\,
  October 2026 to August 2027\n\nMore: https://www.surfacedesign.org/air_fo
 rm/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.surfacedesign.org/air_form/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugra
 ntdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Fully virtual. Open to US-based and international applicants\; cur
 rent SDA membership is required.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:bigci-environmental-awards-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260905T080002Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260802
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260803
SUMMARY:Deadline: BigCi Environmental Awards 2026
DESCRIPTION:BigCi's Environmental Awards are an annual prize for work that 
 engages with the environment\, open to Australian and international artist
 s working in visual art\, installation\, multimedia\, performance\, litera
 ture\, music\, photography\, filmmaking and curating. TWO WINNERS each rec
 eive four weeks of free residency at BigCi plus AUD 5\,000 in prize money.
  BigCi sits on the edge of Wollemi National Park in the Greater Blue Mount
 ains\, an hour and a half from Sydney but geologically another world\; win
 ners explore the UNESCO World Heritage-listed Wollemi\, Blue Mountains and
  Gardens of Stone National Parks on guided walks and are given research as
 sistance relevant to their projects. At the end of their residencies they 
 present the work at the BigCi Open Day. JUDGING: three independent judges\
 , Lea Kannar-Lichtenberger (Australia)\, Brian Wallace (China) and Dan Got
 tlieb (USA). APPLICATION: an online submission form (Wufoo) linked from th
 e awards page\, with a non-refundable AUD 40 entry fee paid at the time of
  submission. KEY DATES: submissions close 2 August 2026\; winners announce
 d 22 August 2026. Enquiries via the contact form at bigci.org/contact.\n\n
 Organisation: Bilpin International Ground for Creative Initiatives (BigCi)
 \nLocation: Bilpin\, New South Wales\, Australia\, on the edge of Wollemi 
 National Park in the UNESCO World Heritage-listed Greater Blue Mountains\,
  about 1.5 hours from Sydney. Open to Australian and international artists
 .\nAward: Two awards\, each of AUD 5\,000 prize money plus four weeks of f
 ree residency at BigCi\, guided walks in the surrounding national parks an
 d research assistance for the project. AUD 40 entry fee\, non-refundable a
 nd payable with the submission.\nDuration: Four-week residency at BigCi pe
 r winner\n\nMore: https://bigci.org/awards/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugr
 antdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://bigci.org/awards/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_med
 ium=referral
LOCATION:Bilpin\, New South Wales\, Australia\, on the edge of Wollemi Nati
 onal Park in the UNESCO World Heritage-listed Greater Blue Mountains\, abo
 ut 1.5 hours from Sydney. Open to Australian and international artists.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:american-academy-berlin-prize-2027-28@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260905T080002Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260925
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260926
SUMMARY:Deadline: American Academy in Berlin: Berlin Prize Fellowship 2027-
 28
DESCRIPTION:The American Academy in Berlin is an independent\, nonprofit in
 stitute for advanced study in the arts\, humanities\, social sciences\, la
 w\, journalism and public policy\, whose primary mission is to strengthen 
 the cultural and intellectual ties between the United States and Germany. 
 Each year\, after a rigorous selection process\, the Academy awards roughl
 y two dozen semester-long Berlin Prize fellowships to individuals who demo
 nstrate excellence in their fields. DISCIPLINES: applications are welcome 
 across the humanities\, social sciences\, law\, journalism and various are
 as of public policy. The Academy generally encourages projects addressing 
 pressing issues of the day\, specifically naming democracy\, security\, te
 chnology and society (especially AI)\, energy including climate\, and glob
 al order. Dedicated fellowships also support projects in Jewish studies\, 
 German studies\, and public health and biotechnology. Project proposals ne
 ed not focus on Germany\, but candidates should explain how a residency in
  Berlin would enhance their work. NOT ACCEPTED: proposals in mathematics o
 r the natural sciences. NOTE FOR ARTISTS: visual artists and composers are
  invited through separate\, nomination-based competitions\, so there is no
  open application route for them here. ELIGIBILITY: fellowships are restri
 cted to citizens and permanent residents of the United States. Both emergi
 ng and established figures are eligible. PhD holders qualify as emerging s
 cholars with a strong record of peer-reviewed work beyond the dissertation
 \; candidates in non-academic fields such as journalism\, filmmaking and p
 olicy need a significant record of publication or production\; fiction and
  nonfiction writers must have published at least one book with a reputable
  press. WHAT FELLOWS DO: fellows present their work to audiences in and ar
 ound Berlin through lectures\, readings\, public discussions\, performance
 s and film screenings\, and should be willing to engage with relevant Germ
 an institutions\, organizations and the media\; these activities are integ
 ral to the Academy's mission of fostering transatlantic dialogue. APPLICAT
 ION MATERIALS: a project proposal of 1\,500 to 1\,750 words plus a one-pag
 e bibliography\; a curriculum vitae of up to 10 pages\; two writing sample
 s totalling no more than 60 pages\; and three letters of recommendation\, 
 which are due 2 October 2026. DEADLINE: Friday 25 September 2026 at 12:00 
 noon Eastern Time (18:00 Central European Time). TIMELINE: all candidates 
 are notified of the competition outcome by email in late March 2027\; the 
 2027-28 Berlin Prize recipients are announced in May 2027.\n\nOrganisation
 : American Academy in Berlin\nLocation: Berlin\, Germany\, at the Hans Arn
 hold Center. RESTRICTED: fellowships are open only to citizens and permane
 nt residents of the United States.\nAward: Monthly stipend of USD 5\,000\,
  plus roundtrip airfare\, accommodation at or near the Hans Arnhold Center
 \, partial board\, in-house library services and professional networking o
 pportunities. Roughly two dozen fellowships awarded each year. No applicat
 ion fee.\nDuration: One full academic semester (about 4 months). Fall runs
  mid-August to mid-December\; spring runs late January to late May.\n\nMor
 e: https://www.americanacademy.de/apply/apply-for-a-fellowship/?utm_source
 =artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.americanacademy.de/apply/apply-for-a-fellowship/?utm_source
 =artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Berlin\, Germany\, at the Hans Arnhold Center. RESTRICTED: fellows
 hips are open only to citizens and permanent residents of the United State
 s.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:latitude-air-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260905T080002Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260901
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260902
SUMMARY:Deadline: LATITUDE Artist in Residence 2027 (Chicago)
DESCRIPTION:The LATITUDE Artist in Residence program (started in 2013) is a
  one-month production residency where 8-10 artists are granted full access
  to LATITUDE's facilities and community to develop their practice. Its foc
 us is supporting artists who want to use LATITUDE's printing and scanning 
 services to expand their practice\, regardless of medium or experience\; p
 ast residents include photographers\, painters\, performance artists\, new
  media artists\, and sculptors. WHAT RESIDENTS RECEIVE: a $1\,000 printing
  stipend\, unlimited scanning\, a personal studio with a calibrated workst
 ation and 24/7 access\, training and guidance on tech from staff\, an orga
 nised public event to present new work\, and lifetime free lab access. LAT
 ITUDE does not offer a housing stipend but will help schedule studio visit
 s during the month. PUBLIC PROGRAMMING: during the residency each artist p
 uts on one community programming event (past examples include panel discus
 sions\, artist talks\, performances\, workshops\, and critiques\; a galler
 y space is available for curation) and is asked to act as a mentor to the 
 community\, sharing insights and creative methods. WHO SHOULD APPLY: artis
 ts interested in production\, education\, and tech experimentation\; no pr
 ior printing experience\, art education\, or media specificity is required
 \, though professionalism and communication are expected. 2027 PANEL COMMI
 TTEE: Yechen Zhao\, B. Ingrid Olson\, Teri Carson. TIMELINE: applications 
 open 1 August 2026 (free before 16 August\, a $20 donation thereafter) and
  close 1 September 2026 at 12pm (noon) CST\; the selection committee revie
 ws in September\, meets to decide finalists in October\, and acceptances a
 re sent early November 2026. Apply at latitudechicago.org/artist-in-reside
 nce.\n\nOrganisation: LATITUDE Chicago\nLocation: Chicago\, Illinois\, USA
 . No housing stipend offered.\nAward: One-month production residency for 8
 -10 artists: a $1\,000 printing stipend\, unlimited scanning\, a personal 
 studio with a calibrated workstation\, staff training and guidance on tech
 \, an organised public event\, and lifetime free lab access. No housing st
 ipend. Free to apply before 16 August 2026\; a $20 donation thereafter.\nD
 uration: One month (residency takes place in 2027)\n\nMore: https://www.la
 titudechicago.org/artist-in-residence?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdes
 k&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.latitudechicago.org/artist-in-residence?utm_source=artifici
 alnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Chicago\, Illinois\, USA. No housing stipend offered.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:cannes-residence-cinema-de-demain-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260905T080002Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260930
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261001
SUMMARY:Deadline: La Résidence du Festival de Cannes (Cinéma de Demain): 
 Screenwriting Residency in Paris
DESCRIPTION:La Résidence of the Festival de Cannes (part of Cinéma de Dem
 ain\, formerly La Cinéfondation) invites 12 directors each year to write 
 their screenplay in Paris. The programme provides a place of residence in 
 the heart of Paris\, a personalised programme accompanying the writing of 
 their scripts\, and a collective programme of forums with film industry pr
 ofessionals. The 12 directors from the two annual sessions present their p
 rojects at the CNC pavilion in Cannes\, giving 10-minute presentations to 
 an audience of professionals and a jury of three film industry personaliti
 es\; the CNC then awards a EUR 5\,000 grant to one director from each sess
 ion\, based on the project that most inspires the jury. The Cinef & Résid
 ence offices serve as a hub where directors meet and schedule private meet
 ings with potential producers. ELIGIBILITY: open to young filmmakers who h
 ave made one or several short films and no more than one feature film\; ca
 ndidates must NOT be residents or citizens of France. Selected filmmakers 
 must stay at the Résidence in Paris for the full session. SESSIONS: two p
 er year\, each lasting four and a half months\, the first running 15 March
  to 31 July and the second 1 October to 15 February. Artistic fields: film
 \, writing\, interdisciplinary. Deadline: 30 September 2026 at 16:00 UTC. 
 Free to apply.\n\nOrganisation: Festival de Cannes (Cinéma de Demain / La
  Résidence)\nLocation: Paris\, France. Candidates must not be residents o
 r citizens of France.\nAward: Fully supported: a place of residence in cen
 tral Paris\, a personalised script-writing programme\, and a collective pr
 ogramme of forums with film industry professionals. Directors present at t
 he CNC pavilion in Cannes\, where the CNC awards a EUR 5\,000 grant to one
  director per session. Free to apply.\nDuration: Four and a half months pe
 r session (two sessions a year)\n\nMore: https://cinemadedemain.festival-c
 annes.com/en/take-part/register-to-the-residence/?utm_source=artificialnou
 veaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://cinemadedemain.festival-cannes.com/en/take-part/register-to-the
 -residence/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Paris\, France. Candidates must not be residents or citizens of Fr
 ance.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:fucina-futura-residency-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260905T080002Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260820
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260821
SUMMARY:Deadline: Fucina Futura 2027 Residency (Marostica\, Italy)
DESCRIPTION:Fucina Futura is an artist residency in Marostica\, a small tow
 n about an hour from Venice\, offering a peaceful setting to develop resea
 rch in close contact with nature and the local community. The project span
 s two locations: the home designed by internationally renowned illustrator
  Elena Xausa near the historic centre\, and the atelier Fucina Ai Gorghi b
 y beloved local painter Daniele Marcon\, ideal for large-scale installatio
 ns. Led by visual artist Lavinia and curator Sofia (Futura Association)\, 
 the program invites residents to develop projects that engage with the com
 munity and the working location\, and encourages artists of any discipline
  to make full use of the large atelier. The central goal is the 'restituti
 on' to citizens\, fostering cultural exchange and community inclusion: thi
 s final phase is tailored to each artist and can take the form of an exhib
 ition\, performance\, workshop\, screening\, talk or informal sharing sess
 ion\, prioritising direct dialogue with the public and the contemporary re
 newal of local tradition. THREE residents are hosted together from 12 Apri
 l to 12 May 2027 and share a spacious house\; they receive a daily fee to 
 cover living expenses\, and travel expenses are also covered. Made possibl
 e by Culture Moves Europe. ELIGIBILITY: only artists residing in one of th
 e Creative Europe countries EXCEPT ITALY may apply. Creative Europe covers
  the 27 EU member states (other than Italy)\, plus Albania\, Armenia\, Bos
 nia and Herzegovina\, Georgia\, Iceland\, Kosovo\, Liechtenstein\, Moldova
 \, Montenegro\, North Macedonia\, Norway\, Serbia\, Tunisia and Ukraine\, 
 and includes the EU's Overseas Countries and Territories and Outermost Reg
 ions (Greenland\; French Guiana\, French Polynesia\, French Southern and A
 ntarctic Territories\, Guadeloupe\, Martinique\, Mayotte\, New Caledonia\,
  Reunion\, Saint Barthelemy\, Saint-Martin\, St. Pierre and Miquelon\, Wal
 lis and Futuna\; Azores\, Madeira\; Canary Islands\; Aruba\, Bonaire\, Cur
 acao\, Saba\, Sint Maarten\, Sint Eustatius). TIMELINE: apply via the Goog
 le Form by 20 August 2026\; selected artists informed by 30 August and mus
 t confirm participation by 8 September 2026.\n\nOrganisation: Futura Assoc
 iation (Fucina Futura)\, supported by Culture Moves Europe\nLocation: Maro
 stica\, Italy (one hour from Venice). Open to artists resident in a Creati
 ve Europe country except Italy.\nAward: Fully funded: daily fee to cover l
 iving expenses\, travel costs covered\, and shared house accommodation pro
 vided (supported by Culture Moves Europe). No application fee.\nDuration: 
 One month\, 12 April to 12 May 2027\; three residents hosted together\n\nM
 ore: https://docs.google.com/forms/d/e/1FAIpQLScwEmQ2EFrMoukgNorlOLE5kGvGr
 iMX0IdMBObmhfgRlSW-jQ/viewform?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_m
 edium=referral
URL:https://docs.google.com/forms/d/e/1FAIpQLScwEmQ2EFrMoukgNorlOLE5kGvGriM
 X0IdMBObmhfgRlSW-jQ/viewform?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_med
 ium=referral
LOCATION:Marostica\, Italy (one hour from Venice). Open to artists resident
  in a Creative Europe country except Italy.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:inlaks-fine-art-award-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260905T080002Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260815
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260816
SUMMARY:Deadline: Inlaks Shivdasani Foundation: Inlaks Fine Art Award 2027
DESCRIPTION:A one-year award enabling an emerging Indian visual artist to p
 ursue independent practice\, with a stipulation to attend a recognised art
  residency programme in India for four weeks during the year. ELIGIBILITY 
 IS RESTRICTED: Indian citizens currently living in India\, born on or afte
 r 1 January 1996\, who are either recent graduates\, postgraduates or fina
 l-year students of a leading Indian visual arts institute\, or artists wit
 hout those formal credentials but with a record of exhibition participatio
 n. First-year MA students are not eligible. Awardees submit progress repor
 ts at six months and on completion\, plus documentation of the residency. 
 Applications close 15 August 2026 at 23:59 IST via Submittable. Shortlisti
 ng is expected in late October 2026 with interviews in early November.\n\n
 Organisation: Inlaks Shivdasani Foundation\nLocation: India. Open to India
 n citizens currently living in India.\nAward: INR 300\,000 (around USD 3\,
 400 / EUR 2\,950) for one year\, plus a fully covered four-week art reside
 ncy at an Indian art institution. No application fee.\nDuration: Twelve mo
 nths of independent practice\, including a mandatory four-week residency w
 ithin India.\n\nMore: https://inlaksfoundation.submittable.com/submit/75e1
 4093-df9b-41c5-af96-ce2360e64e1b/the-inlaks-fine-art-award-2027?utm_source
 =artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://inlaksfoundation.submittable.com/submit/75e14093-df9b-41c5-af96
 -ce2360e64e1b/the-inlaks-fine-art-award-2027?utm_source=artificialnouveaug
 rantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:India. Open to Indian citizens currently living in India.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:instituto-sacatar-fellowship-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260905T080002Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260828
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260829
SUMMARY:Deadline: Instituto Sacatar: Sacatar Fellowship 2027 (Bahia\, Brazi
 l)
DESCRIPTION:Brazil's longest-running artist residency\, on the island of It
 aparica across the bay from Salvador\, hosting six to eight artists per se
 ssion for roughly eight weeks. Open to all creative disciplines and\, unus
 ually for this region\, to all nationalities\, which makes it one of the f
 ew genuinely international residencies in Latin America. Sacatar provides 
 full hosting (private room\, three meals daily\, individual studio\, laund
 ry\, local logistics and connections) but explicitly does NOT provide a st
 ipend\, airfare to Salvador\, art materials or discretionary local expense
 s\, so applicants must fund their own travel. Individual artists only\; gr
 oup proposals are not accepted and former Sacatar Fellows may not reapply.
  COVID vaccination is required. NOTE: there is a USD 25 application fee. A
 pply through SlideRoom by 28 August 2026. Contact info@sacatar.org.\n\nOrg
 anisation: Instituto Sacatar\nLocation: Itaparica island\, Bahia\, Brazil.
  Open to all nationalities.\nAward: Fully hosted residency: private bedroo
 m with attached bathroom\, three meals a day (except Saturday evenings\, S
 undays and Brazilian holidays)\, an individual studio suited to the discip
 line\, weekly laundry\, logistical support and airport transfer from Salva
 dor. NO STIPEND\, no airfare and no materials budget. USD 25 application f
 ee. One French artist may additionally receive airfare and a stipend throu
 gh Villa Fatumbi.\nDuration: Approximately eight weeks\; specific session 
 dates are listed in the application.\n\nMore: https://sacatar.org/en/fello
 wships/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://sacatar.org/en/fellowships/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantde
 sk&utm_medium=referral
