UID:francoise-cite-internationale-arts-clinique-2027@artificialnouveau.gith
 ub.io
DTSTAMP:20260905T080002Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260816
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260817
SUMMARY:Deadline: Association Francoise pour l'oeuvre contemporaine x Cite 
 internationale des arts: Research and Creation Residency 2027
DESCRIPTION:Launched in 2025\, this research and creation residency explore
 s the links between contemporary art\, psychiatry and the human sciences. 
 It is expressly not a therapeutic programme. It seeks artists whose practi
 ce resonates with the notion of the clinical\, meaning a situated approach
  that works closely with lived narratives and specific contexts\, and that
  engages with questions of care. The selected artist receives tailored sup
 port from Albertine de Galbert\, a clinical psychologist and exhibition cu
 rator\, including four to six working sessions\, alongside networking and 
 meeting time with programme partners. Outputs include an ongoing online jo
 urnal\, a mid-residency open studio\, and a closing presentation (exhibiti
 on\, public talk or publication) defined with the artist. Applications go 
 by email as a single PDF of no more than 15 MB to residence@francoiseartme
 mo.fr\, and require a two-page CV\, a ten-page portfolio\, a two-page stat
 ement of intent\, and a letter of recommendation from an institution or a 
 curator. A jury of four to six art and psychiatry specialists decides\, wi
 th results announced in September 2026.\n\nOrganisation: Association Franc
 oise pour l'oeuvre contemporaine / Cite internationale des arts\nLocation:
  Paris\, France (Marais site of the Cite internationale des arts). Open to
  artist-authors of any nationality and any discipline\, mid-career or esta
 blished\, with no age limit. Applicants must be comfortable working in Fre
 nch or English. Note: the studio-apartments do not meet current accessibil
 ity standards for people with disabilities.\nAward: Grant of EUR 1\,000 ne
 t of tax per month\, plus a furnished studio-apartment (20 to 35 sq m\, co
 mbining living and working space)\, international return travel to Paris a
 irports or stations\, travel insurance\, and capped research support again
 st receipts. Additional fees apply for accompanying persons. No applicatio
 n fee.\nDuration: Approximately 12 weeks\, 5 January to 26 March 2027\n\nM
 ore: https://www.citeinternationaledesarts.fr/appels-a-candidature/associa
 tion-francoise-pour-loeuvre-contemporaine-x-cite-internationale-des-arts/?
 utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.citeinternationaledesarts.fr/appels-a-candidature/associati
 on-francoise-pour-loeuvre-contemporaine-x-cite-internationale-des-arts/?ut
 m_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Paris\, France (Marais site of the Cite internationale des arts). 
 Open to artist-authors of any nationality and any discipline\, mid-career 
 or established\, with no age limit. Applicants must be comfortable working
  in French or English. Note: the studio-apartments do not meet current acc
 essibility standards for people with disabilities.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:bloedel-reserve-creative-residency-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260905T080002Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260901
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260902
SUMMARY:Deadline: Creative Residency at Bloedel Reserve: Class of 2027
DESCRIPTION:Bloedel Reserve's residency programme exists to foster creative
  thinking inspired by nature and to explore the connection between humans 
 and the environment. It is open to professional and emerging artists\, com
 posers\, writers\, botanists\, landscape architects\, researchers and othe
 rs working on themes of nature and human connection to it. Residents get a
  three-week stay at a 140-acre internationally recognised public garden an
 d forest preserve\, housed in a small home hidden in the woods off the mai
 n paths. Note for non-US applicants: the funding is structured as expense 
 reimbursement rather than a stipend\, capped at USD 1\,000\, which for tra
 nsatlantic applicants is unlikely to cover airfare plus meals over three w
 eeks.\n\nOrganisation: Bloedel Reserve\nLocation: Bainbridge Island\, Wash
 ington\, USA\, between Seattle and the Kitsap Peninsula\, reachable by fer
 ry from Seattle or by car via the Kitsap Peninsula. Residents must be 21 o
 r older. International applicants are eligible but are funded differently 
 from domestic ones (see amount).\nAward: USD 22 application fee. Furnished
  private house on the grounds with linens\, stocked kitchen\, wi-fi\, laun
 dry and use of a smart car\; an electric piano is available. Domestic resi
 dents receive a USD 1\,000 stipend. International residents receive no sti
 pend and may instead submit up to USD 1\,000 of expenses for reimbursement
 . Residents pay for their own meals and their own travel to Bainbridge Isl
 and. There is no dedicated studio\; residents work in the house.\nDuration
 : Three weeks\; residents stay one at a time with no overlap\n\nMore: http
 s://bloedelreserve.slideroom.com/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&ut
 m_medium=referral#/login/program/88209
URL:https://bloedelreserve.slideroom.com/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrant
 desk&utm_medium=referral#/login/program/88209
LOCATION:Bainbridge Island\, Washington\, USA\, between Seattle and the Kit
 sap Peninsula\, reachable by ferry from Seattle or by car via the Kitsap P
 eninsula. Residents must be 21 or older. International applicants are elig
 ible but are funded differently from domestic ones (see amount).
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:k3-tanzplan-hamburg-choreography-residency-2027-2028@artificialnouveau.
 github.io
DTSTAMP:20260905T080002Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260812
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260813
SUMMARY:Deadline: K3 | Tanzplan Hamburg: Choreography Residency 2027-2028
DESCRIPTION:K3 in Hamburg is one of the better-funded choreography residenc
 ies in Europe\, and it is aimed squarely at people who want research time 
 rather than a production deadline. Three choreographers are selected for e
 ight months at Kampnagel with a monthly scholarship\, studio access\, a pr
 oduction budget\, and dramaturgical\, technical and PR support\, plus outr
 each work and a public presentation at the end. Selection runs in two stag
 es. ELIGIBILITY: individual choreographers at an emerging to early mid-car
 eer stage who have already produced independent choreographic work outside
  an educational context. Open internationally\; formal dance training is n
 ot required. Teams and ensembles are not eligible\, only individuals. Appl
 ications close 12 August 2026 at 16:00 CEST.\n\nOrganisation: K3 | Tanzpla
 n Hamburg (Kampnagel)\nLocation: Hamburg\, Germany. Open internationally.\
 nAward: Monthly scholarship (EUR 2\,500 per month in the 2025/26 cycle\; t
 he 2027/28 figure had not been announced at the time of listing)\, plus on
 e-time travel expenses\, visa assistance\, a moderate production budget\, 
 studio access\, and dramaturgical\, technical and PR support. No applicati
 on fee is stated.\nDuration: Eight months\, August 2027 to April 2028\n\nM
 ore: https://www.k3-hamburg.de/en/k3/jobs&callforproposals?utm_source=arti
 ficialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.k3-hamburg.de/en/k3/jobs&callforproposals?utm_source=artifi
 cialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Hamburg\, Germany. Open internationally.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:new-fears-berlin-performance-art-award-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260905T080002Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260815
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260816
SUMMARY:Deadline: NEW FEARS Gallery: Berlin Performance Art Award 2026
DESCRIPTION:NEW FEARS is a Berlin gallery set up as a hybrid space for arti
 sts working across choreography and visual art\, with a focus on body-base
 d practice\, and this award extends that to artists working outside conven
 tional theatre and institutional structures. A jury of Bryana Fritz\, Jaco
 po Lanteri and Burcu Bilgic shortlists six projects\, each of which perfor
 ms live at the gallery on 16 or 17 October 2026\; three winners are then c
 hosen and celebrated at Panke Club on 18 October. The application is unusu
 ally light: name\, email\, an optional text of up to 500 words about your 
 work\, and up to six links to video documentation with passwords where nee
 ded. No CV\, no written proposal\, no budget\, which means the video docum
 entation carries the whole application\, so lead with work that shows imme
 diately how it operates live in a gallery. ELIGIBILITY: Berlin-based artis
 ts\, duos and groups working across performance\, choreography\, visual ar
 t or body-based practice. You must be in Berlin for setup the week before 
 and for the presentations on 16-17 October 2026\, and be able to issue an 
 invoice for the award payment. Existing or new work is fine\, with no stat
 ed duration limits. AVAILABLE EQUIPMENT: sound system\, speakers\, mixer\,
  LED lights\, projector\, wind machine\, smoke machine\, microphone and ba
 sic cables\; check the venue PDF before proposing anything that depends on
  a particular room\, access route or technical setup. NOTE: the organisers
  describe this as a DIY\, near-zero-budget initiative. There is no product
 ion budget and nothing beyond the grant\, so a group splitting EUR 1\,300 
 should go in with eyes open. TIMELINE: applications close 15 August 2026 (
 no time or timezone given)\; shortlist announced 15 September 2026. Apply 
 through the JotForm at form.jotform.com/261674665583066.\n\nOrganisation: 
 NEW FEARS Gallery\nLocation: Berlin\, Germany (NEW FEARS Gallery\, Reichen
 bergerstrasse 114\, 10999 Berlin). Berlin-based applicants only.\nAward: T
 hree winning projects each receive a EUR 1\,300 grant\, shared between the
  members of a duo or group\, plus a ten-day residency at NEW FEARS Gallery
 \, basic technical support and access to the gallery's equipment. The thre
 e shortlisted projects that do not win divide 50 percent of the entrance r
 evenue equally. Travel\, accommodation\, materials\, shipping\, insurance\
 , per diem and production costs are NOT covered\, and artists with a non-G
 erman tax number may see tax deducted from the prize at an unstated rate. 
 No application fee.\nDuration: Ten-day residency scheduled with the winner
 s during 2026 or 2027\; setup the week before 16 October\, public performa
 nces 16-17 October 2026\n\nMore: https://www.newfears.net/?utm_source=arti
 ficialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.newfears.net/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_med
 ium=referral
LOCATION:Berlin\, Germany (NEW FEARS Gallery\, Reichenbergerstrasse 114\, 1
 0999 Berlin). Berlin-based applicants only.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:drift-choreographer-open-call-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260905T080002Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260831
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260901
SUMMARY:Deadline: DRIFT: Choreographer Open Call 2026-27 (Dance and Researc
 h In The Future Time)
DESCRIPTION:DRIFT (Dance and Research In The Future Time) is a four-year Cr
 eative Europe cooperation between ten European organisations\, led by Thea
 tre de Liege and Maison de la danse. The distinguishing feature is that it
  funds artistic research rather than production: you are expected to arriv
 e with questions\, not a finished piece you already intend to make\, and r
 eviewers read every section of the application through that lens. STRUCTUR
 E: each of the ten partner organisations selects one choreographer from it
 s own country\, forming a cohort of ten. Over the programme you get an Onb
 oarding Lab\, two weeks of residency with your national partner\, three co
 llaborative Online Labs\, a Festival Lab in Tunis\, individual artistic an
 d curatorial mentoring\, and a work-in-progress showcase at the Biennale d
 e la danse in Lyon\, all backed by a EUR 20\,000 research budget that cove
 rs your fees\, travel\, accommodation and any collaborators. SECOND PHASE:
  after a further evaluation\, five of the ten artists may receive co-produ
 ction support and touring across the DRIFT network. That phase is separate
  from this application and is not guaranteed. ELIGIBILITY: professional ch
 oreographers in contemporary dance or performance\, at a mid-career stage\
 , meaning an established national practice but not yet fully established i
 nternationally. You must be based in Belgium\, France\, Germany (Berlin re
 gion only)\, Greece\, Norway\, Italy\, Spain\, Ukraine\, Tunisia or Portug
 al\, and you apply through the partner organisation representing your coun
 try of residence\, not centrally. Good English and availability for all pr
 ogramme dates are required. The country list is strict\, so check it befor
 e you start. TIMELINE: applications opened 1 July 2026 and close 31 August
  2026\; selection through September\; results 9 October 2026\; programme r
 uns November 2026 to September 2027.\n\nOrganisation: DRIFT\, a four-year 
 Creative Europe cooperation of ten organisations led by Theatre de Liege a
 nd Maison de la danse\nLocation: Activities across Europe: kick-off in Ber
 lin\, Festival Lab in Tunis\, showcase in Lyon\, online labs\, plus a resi
 dency hosted by your own national DRIFT partner. Applicants must be based 
 in Belgium\, France\, Germany (Berlin region only)\, Greece\, Norway\, Ita
 ly\, Spain\, Ukraine\, Tunisia or Portugal\, and apply through the partner
  organisation for their country of residence.\nAward: EUR 20\,000 research
  budget per choreographer\, covering artistic fees\, travel\, accommodatio
 n and optional collaborator costs. Also included: an Onboarding Lab\, two 
 weeks of residency with your national DRIFT partner\, three collaborative 
 Online Labs\, a Festival Lab in Tunis\, individual artistic mentoring and 
 individual curatorial mentoring\, and a work-in-progress presentation at t
 he Biennale de la danse in Lyon. No application fee.\nDuration: November 2
 026 to September 2027. Kick-off in Berlin 30 November to 1 December 2026\;
  Festival Lab in Tunis 18-21 March 2027\; Biennale de la danse showcase in
  Lyon 18-25 September 2027.\n\nMore: https://driftdance.eu/artists/open-ca
 ll?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://driftdance.eu/artists/open-call?utm_source=artificialnouveaugra
 ntdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Activities across Europe: kick-off in Berlin\, Festival Lab in Tun
 is\, showcase in Lyon\, online labs\, plus a residency hosted by your own 
 national DRIFT partner. Applicants must be based in Belgium\, France\, Ger
 many (Berlin region only)\, Greece\, Norway\, Italy\, Spain\, Ukraine\, Tu
 nisia or Portugal\, and apply through the partner organisation for their c
 ountry of residence.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:screen-argyll-sea-change-animated-women-2026@artificialnouveau.github.i
 o
DTSTAMP:20260905T080002Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260810
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260811
SUMMARY:Deadline: Screen Argyll: Sea Change - Animated Women 2026-27 (envir
 onmental animation development)
DESCRIPTION:Sea Change is a seven-month professional development programme 
 from Screen Argyll for six UK-based women and marginalised gender animator
 s developing animated feature film projects\, with environmental storytell
 ing at the centre of the brief. It runs a project from concept toward scre
 en: online workshops in early September 2026\, a residential lab on the Is
 le of Tiree from 15 to 22 September 2026\, monthly mentoring from October 
 2026 to February 2027\, a project showcase at MOVE Summit in February 2027
 \, and final submissions in March 2027. WHAT PARTICIPANTS GET: GBP 150 per
  day for 20 days of participation\, which comes to GBP 3\,000\; accommodat
 ion covered during the residential lab\; travel support\; and industry pas
 ses for MOVE Summit and Glasgow Film Festival 2027. ELIGIBILITY: UK reside
 nt\, over 18\, a woman or marginalised gender animator\, not in full-time 
 education when the programme begins\, with prior experience making short-f
 orm or longer animation\, and a feature project at early-to-mid developmen
 t stage. NOTE THE TIMING: the deadline is 10 August 2026 and selected proj
 ects are notified on 17 August 2026\, so this is a short-turnaround applic
 ation.\n\nOrganisation: Screen Argyll\nLocation: UK-based applicants. Onli
 ne workshops plus a residential lab on the Isle of Tiree\, Scotland\, and 
 a showcase at MOVE Summit\, Edinburgh.\nAward: GBP 150 per day for 20 days
  of participation (GBP 3\,000 total)\, plus covered accommodation during t
 he residential lab\, travel support\, and industry passes for MOVE Summit 
 and Glasgow Film Festival 2027. No application fee.\nDuration: Seven month
 s\, September 2026 to March 2027. Deadline 10 August 2026\; selections ann
 ounced 17 August 2026.\n\nMore: https://screenargyll.co.uk/sea-change-anim
 ated-women/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://screenargyll.co.uk/sea-change-animated-women/?utm_source=artifi
 cialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:UK-based applicants. Online workshops plus a residential lab on th
 e Isle of Tiree\, Scotland\, and a showcase at MOVE Summit\, Edinburgh.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:titanik-mcult-residing-non-linear-technologies-sonic-worldings-2027@art
 ificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260905T080002Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260830
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260831
SUMMARY:Deadline: Residing Non-linear Technologies and Sonic Worldings (M-C
 ult x Titanik\, Helsinki and Turku)
DESCRIPTION:A new pilot residency from Art and Media Culture Agency M-Cult 
 in Helsinki and Arte artists' association / Titanik gallery in Turku\, sit
 ting at the intersection of art\, media and sonic practice\, funded by the
  Nordic-Baltic Mobility Programme for Culture from Nordic Culture Point. T
 WO ARTISTS will be selected. THE PREMISE: the programme treats technology 
 as a situated practice and deliberately shifts attention away from the pro
 gress narratives promoted by large global technology companies\, toward lo
 cal\, indigenous and community-oriented perspectives. It focuses on making
  vernacular and non-linear technological and sonic environments visible\, 
 foregrounding vibrant local histories and traditions across the Nordic and
  Baltic region\, and asks what non-linear sonic and media practice might l
 ook like in the current social\, political and cultural landscape. WHAT EA
 CH ARTIST RECEIVES: EUR 3\,500 artist fee\; EUR 2\,000 production budget\;
  accommodation for two months divided between Helsinki and Turku\, one mon
 th in each\; travel costs for one return ticket from a Nordic-Baltic count
 ry\; access to Titanik's residency studio space and a working desk at M-Cu
 lt's office during each stay\; mentoring and curatorial support from both 
 organisations' staff\; and networking into the Finnish media art and conte
 mporary art scenes. In return the artist develops in-depth or focused rese
 arch and presents their practice and research at two public events\, one i
 n each city. WHO CAN APPLY: individual artists only\, explicitly NOT colle
 ctives this round\; artists from or based in Finland\, Sweden\, Norway\, D
 enmark\, Iceland\, Aland\, the Faroe Islands\, Greenland\, Estonia\, Latvi
 a or Lithuania\; working in sound\, media and/or digital arts\, or the exp
 anded field of vernacular technologies\, all defined broadly by the organi
 sers. Applicants must be available to travel to Finland for the full two-m
 onth period across February and March 2027. The organisers welcome applica
 nts at all career stages and from all backgrounds. One submission per arti
 st: if two are sent\, only the later one is reviewed. HOW TO APPLY: submit
  via the form linked from the open call page with a brief letter of motiva
 tion including a research plan for the residency\, how it connects with th
 e programme's themes and what you would focus on (max 1-2 pages)\, your CV
 \, and a link to your portfolio. Applications in English only. DEADLINE: 3
 0 August 2026 at 23:59 UTC+3. Online interviews with shortlisted artists t
 ake place before 19 September 2026\, and all applicants are notified by em
 ail by the end of September 2026. JURY: Jussi Koitela\, Director of M-Cult
 \, Helsinki\, and Farbod Fakharzadeh\, Director of Arte ry and Titanik\, T
 urku. ACCOMMODATION AND ACCESSIBILITY: in Helsinki\, the Vallila Residency
  hosted by M-Cult is a 30 sqm apartment in a small wooden house with one r
 oom\, kitchen and bathroom with shower\; it is NOT fully accessible\, sitt
 ing on the first floor up stairs with no elevator (details from info@m-cul
 t.org). In Turku\, the Titanik A.i.R. apartment is 27 sqm on the top floor
  of a building with an elevator\, but the space including the bathroom is 
 not fully accessible (details from info@titanik.fi). A partner\, friend or
  child may join\, but their expenses are not covered. THE PARTNERS: M-Cult
 \, founded in 2000\, develops and presents art and media culture with a fo
 cus on the social and political dimensions of media and technology\, worki
 ng through art commissions\, residencies and learning programmes. Titanik\
 , established in Turku in 1988 by the artist-run organization Arte Ry\, ha
 s since 2006 run Titanik A.i.R.\, the first artist residency in Finland wi
 th a focus on sound art and sonic practices. CONTACT: Jussi Koitela (jussi
 .koitela@m-cult.org) or Farbod Fakharzadeh (director@arte.fi).\n\nOrganisa
 tion: Art and Media Culture Agency M-Cult (Helsinki) and Arte artists' ass
 ociation / Titanik gallery (Turku)\nLocation: Finland\; one month in Helsi
 nki and one month in Turku. Open to artists from or based in Nordic and Ba
 ltic countries.\nAward: EUR 3\,500 artist fee plus EUR 2\,000 production b
 udget\, two months' accommodation split between Helsinki and Turku\, and o
 ne return travel ticket from a Nordic-Baltic country. No application fee s
 tated.\nDuration: Two months\, February to March 2027 (one month in each c
 ity)\n\nMore: https://www.titanik.fi/residency-open-call-residing-non-line
 ar-technologies-and-sonic-worldings/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk
 &utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.titanik.fi/residency-open-call-residing-non-linear-technolo
 gies-and-sonic-worldings/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium
 =referral
LOCATION:Finland\; one month in Helsinki and one month in Turku. Open to ar
 tists from or based in Nordic and Baltic countries.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:kluge-fellowships-library-of-congress-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260905T080002Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260915
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260916
SUMMARY:Deadline: Kluge Fellowships at the Library of Congress 2027
DESCRIPTION:The John W. Kluge Center awards twelve residential fellowships 
 a year for humanistic and social science research that draws on the Librar
 y of Congress collections\, with interdisciplinary and cross-cultural proj
 ects particularly welcome. Fellows get four to eleven months in residence 
 at a stipend of USD 5\,000 per month\, work and discussion space in the Je
 fferson Building\, and access to the Library's specialist staff\, the worl
 d's largest law library\, extensive multilingual books and periodicals\, a
 nd deep special collections of manuscripts\, maps\, music\, films\, record
 ed sound\, prints and photographs\, alongside the wider scholarly communit
 y in Washington. The Center was established in 2000 through a USD 60 milli
 on endowment from John W. Kluge. ELIGIBILITY: scholars who received a term
 inal advanced degree (the highest degree available in the discipline\, usu
 ally a PhD\, JD or international equivalent) within the past seven years\,
  in the humanities\, social sciences\, or a professional field such as arc
 hitecture or law. The degree must be in hand at the time of submission\, a
 nd full-time students are therefore out of scope. Open to U.S. citizens an
 d foreign nationals\; selected foreign nationals are assisted in obtaining
  a Library-sponsored J-1 visa\, though holders of H-1B visas are not eligi
 ble. APPLICATION: through the Library's online portal\, opening 15 April 2
 026 and closing 11:59pm on 15 September 2026. Materials required are an ap
 plication form in English\, a CV (max 2 pages)\, a 500-word research abstr
 act\, a one-page statement on how the Library's collections will serve the
  work\, a project proposal with work plan (max 3 pages)\, a bibliography (
 max 3 pages)\, and two reference letters from referees who have read the p
 roposal. No fee.\n\nOrganisation: John W. Kluge Center\, Library of Congre
 ss\nLocation: Washington\, DC\, United States (in residence at the Thomas 
 Jefferson Building\, 101 Independence Ave SE). Open to applicants of any n
 ationality: U.S. citizens and foreign nationals alike\, with J-1 exchange 
 visitor visa sponsorship provided for those who need it.\nAward: Stipend o
 f USD 5\,000 per month for a residency of four to eleven months\, so USD 2
 0\,000 to USD 55\,000 depending on the tenure awarded. Twelve Kluge Fellow
 ships are awarded each year. Stipends are paid monthly by electronic trans
 fer to a U.S. bank account. No application fee\, no participation fee.\nDu
 ration: Four to eleven months in residence\; fellows may take up residence
  at any point in the 18-month window after receiving the fellowship letter
 \n\nMore: https://www.loc.gov/programs/john-w-kluge-center/chairs-fellowsh
 ips/fellowships/kluge-fellowships/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&u
 tm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.loc.gov/programs/john-w-kluge-center/chairs-fellowships/fel
 lowships/kluge-fellowships/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medi
 um=referral
LOCATION:Washington\, DC\, United States (in residence at the Thomas Jeffer
 son Building\, 101 Independence Ave SE). Open to applicants of any nationa
 lity: U.S. citizens and foreign nationals alike\, with J-1 exchange visito
 r visa sponsorship provided for those who need it.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:mpi-empirical-aesthetics-inhabit-16-17-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260905T080002Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260901
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260902
SUMMARY:Deadline: INHABIT #16 and #17: Artist-in-Residence 2027 (Max Planck
  Institute for Empirical Aesthetics)
DESCRIPTION:INHABIT is the artist-in-residence programme of the Max Planck 
 Institute for Empirical Aesthetics in Frankfurt am Main\, which invites ar
 tists from various disciplines to collaborate with its scientists and rese
 archers\, treating artistic thinking and knowledge as reflective practices
  in their own right. The 2027 call is addressed to artists from the perfor
 ming arts and music\, who spend four months creating new work\, or develop
 ing an existing project\, inside a scientific research environment and in 
 dialogue with the institute's researchers. Two slots are open for 2027: IN
 HABIT #16 from April to July 2027 and INHABIT #17 from September to Decemb
 er 2027. Work developed during the residency is presented in cooperation w
 ith local arts institutions as an exhibition\, performance\, concert or ot
 her format\, complemented by discursive formats. WHO THEY ARE LOOKING FOR:
  artists from the performing arts and music interested in interdisciplinar
 y collaboration between artistic and scientific fields\; artists open to d
 eveloping and sharing a project as an exhibition\, performance\, concert o
 r another format\; and artists active in contemporary culture who respond 
 to the institute with innovative and challenging ideas. WHAT IS OFFERED: a
 n artist's fee of EUR 8\,000\, an accommodation fee of EUR 2\,000\, a proj
 ect-dependent production budget\, travel costs to and in Frankfurt\, reali
 sation of the final project\, collaborations with scientists\, access to t
 he library\, sound studio and scientific facilities\, and curatorial suppo
 rt during the residency. APPLY through the institute's e-recruiting portal
  by 1 September 2026\, 23:59 CET\; the jury decision follows in November 2
 026. No fee.\n\nOrganisation: Max Planck Institute for Empirical Aesthetic
 s\nLocation: Frankfurt am Main\, Germany\nAward: Artist's fee of EUR 8\,00
 0 plus an accommodation fee of EUR 2\,000\, a production budget depending 
 on the project\, and travel costs to and within Frankfurt. Also included: 
 realisation of the final project\, collaborations with scientists\, access
  to the institute's library\, sound studio and scientific facilities\, and
  curatorial support throughout the residency. No application fee.\nDuratio
 n: Four months. Two slots: INHABIT #16 runs April to July 2027\, INHABIT #
 17 runs September to December 2027\n\nMore: https://www.aesthetics.mpg.de/
 en/research/inhabit-artist-in-residence.html?utm_source=artificialnouveaug
 rantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.aesthetics.mpg.de/en/research/inhabit-artist-in-residence.h
 tml?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Frankfurt am Main\, Germany
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:tartu-city-of-literature-residency-autumn-2026@artificialnouveau.github
 .io
DTSTAMP:20260905T080002Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260901
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260902
SUMMARY:Deadline: Tartu UNESCO City of Literature Residency for Writers and
  Translators\, Autumn 2026
DESCRIPTION:The Estonian Literary Society\, which coordinates Tartu UNESCO 
 City of Literature\, runs an international residency programme for writers
  and translators that has hosted authors from Slovenia\, Ireland\, Hungary
 \, the USA\, the UK\, Ukraine and Canada since 2017. This is the open call
  for the autumn 2026 slot: one resident stays two months in the Karl Risti
 kivi residency apartment in Tartu\, working on their own writing or transl
 ation while connecting with the local literary scene. WHAT THE RESIDENCY O
 FFERS: a EUR 600 monthly stipend\, travel reimbursement up to EUR 350\, fr
 ee accommodation\, a local support coordinator to help arrange cultural en
 gagements\, and opportunities to perform and present at literary events. T
 he autumn period covers the interdisciplinary literary festival Crazy Tart
 u on 12 to 14 November\, and the resident is offered a slot to perform the
 re alongside writers\, poets\, musicians and artists from Estonia and abro
 ad. ELIGIBILITY: open to writers\, poets and translators of Estonian liter
 ature of any nationality\; applicants need an upper-intermediate level of 
 spoken English\, at least one published book\, screenplay\, theatre script
  or translation from Estonian into another language\, a demonstrable inter
 est in Estonian culture and literature\, a willingness to take part in loc
 al literary activities\, and a commitment to working on their own literary
  project during the stay. HOW TO APPLY: complete the online application fo
 rm linked from the residency page and upload a fragment of up to five page
 s of a published text (in Estonian or English) or of a published translati
 on. SELECTION: made by a committee representing the Estonian Literary Soci
 ety\, the Estonian Writers Union\, the Estonian Literary Museum and the Ta
 rtu Literature House Foundation. Results are announced before 15 September
  2026. The programme's stated aims are to enhance international exchange\,
  diversify the local literary scene\, introduce new foreign authors and th
 eir work to Estonian readers\, and build lasting connections beyond the re
 sidency period. Questions: Marja Unt\, eks@kirjandus.ee. Deadline 1 Septem
 ber 2026.\n\nOrganisation: Estonian Literary Society (Eesti Kirjanduse Sel
 ts)\, Focal Point for Tartu UNESCO City of Literature\nLocation: Tartu\, E
 stonia. Open to writers\, poets and translators of Estonian literature wor
 ldwide.\nAward: Monthly stipend of EUR 600 for the two-month stay (EUR 1\,
 200 in total)\, plus reimbursement of travel costs up to EUR 350. Free acc
 ommodation is provided in the Karl Ristikivi residency apartment\, which h
 as a bedroom\, living room\, kitchen\, bathroom and exhibition room. One r
 esident is selected. No application fee.\nDuration: Two months\, 15 Octobe
 r to 15 December 2026\, with some flexibility on exact dates\n\nMore: http
 s://tartu.kirjandus.ee/residency-autumn-2026?utm_source=artificialnouveaug
 rantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://tartu.kirjandus.ee/residency-autumn-2026?utm_source=artificialn
 ouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Tartu\, Estonia. Open to writers\, poets and translators of Estoni
 an literature worldwide.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:new-public-entrepreneur-in-residence-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260905T080002Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260909
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260910
SUMMARY:Deadline: New_ Public Entrepreneur in Residence\, Pilot Cohort
DESCRIPTION:New_ Public is piloting a virtual Entrepreneur in Residence pro
 gramme for solo founders building alternatives to the mainstream social pl
 atforms. Its framing: people are turning away from algorithmic social medi
 a\, AI bots are taking over the big platforms\, and users are retreating i
 nto private closed spaces\, which leaves an opening for builders working o
 n a new information ecosystem rather than the status quo. The residency su
 pplies research\, experts\, connections and tools\; the founder brings the
  vision and experience. TWO FOCUS AREAS: humans connecting to humans\, mea
 ning products that bring people into meaningful contact online (forums\, m
 essaging apps\, groups\, microblogging\, in-person meetups)\, including AI
 -powered platforms and tools as long as they connect people to people rath
 er than people to bots\; and local and civic information environments\, me
 aning tools for distributing local news\, building community knowledge and
  facilitating collective sensemaking\, so people can get trustworthy infor
 mation about their communities. WHAT RESIDENTS DO: use self-directed time 
 to prototype ideas\, test assumptions and gain clarity\; participate in re
 gular check-ins\, engage with the other resident and the wider team\, and 
 take candid feedback on product and strategy\; engage seriously with go-to
 -market questions about who the user is\, how they find you\, and the path
  to sustainability\; and help shape the programme itself\, since the pilot
  cohort's feedback directly informs how the EIR programme runs going forwa
 rd. WHO THEY WANT: entrepreneurial founders ready to build their next thin
 g within the focus areas\, with a track record of or clear drive toward wo
 rk that builds social trust\, connection and belonging\; strong product th
 inkers with good judgment\, a relentless focus on user needs\, and opennes
 s to coaching on mission-aligned business models. An early idea is enough\
 , no working product required. The pilot is open to founders with somethin
 g else going on alongside\, though the full programme will most likely be 
 full time. APPLICATION PROCESS: submit the written application online\; sh
 ortlisted applicants submit a short video by 18 September 2026 covering ba
 ckground\, motivation and a quick pitch\; full applications and videos go 
 to the advisory board\, with offers made in early October ahead of the kic
 koff. FUTURE COHORTS: New_ Public plans to open the next cohort to the UK 
 and EU from spring 2027. Deadline 11:59pm Pacific on Wednesday 9 September
  2026.\n\nOrganisation: New_ Public\nLocation: Remote\, with an in-person 
 kickoff retreat in Philadelphia\, USA (19 to 22 October 2026). Open to app
 licants based in the United States or Canada.\nAward: Stipend of USD 22\,5
 00\, paid in three monthly installments across the residency\, each contin
 gent on continued active participation. Plus USD 1\,500 toward an AI tool 
 of the resident's choice for coding\, prototyping or design. Two residents
  are selected in the pilot cohort. Also provided: hands-on product and str
 ategy support from senior New_ Public staff and outside experts\; access t
 o New_ Public's five years of original research on moderation\, governance
 \, community stewardship and platform design\, plus direct access to the r
 esearchers behind it\; and introductions across New_ Public's network of m
 ajor platforms\, startups\, university researchers and funders for hiring\
 , fundraising\, early user testing and community partnerships. Residents m
 ust submit a Form W-9 or W-8BEN to receive the stipend. No application fee
 .\nDuration: Three months\, from the week of 19 October 2026 to 29 January
  2027\, run remotely apart from the opening retreat\n\nMore: https://newpu
 blic.org/EIR?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://newpublic.org/EIR?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_med
 ium=referral
LOCATION:Remote\, with an in-person kickoff retreat in Philadelphia\, USA (
 19 to 22 October 2026). Open to applicants based in the United States or C
 anada.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:archipel-butor-livre-de-dialogue-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260905T080002Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260830
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260831
SUMMARY:Deadline: Archipel Butor Livre de dialogue Creation Residency 2027
DESCRIPTION:Archipel Butor runs its creation residency out of the former ho
 me of the writer Michel Butor in Lucinges\, Haute-Savoie\, and this call i
 s for a livre de dialogue\, a dialogue book made jointly by a writer and a
  visual artist. The residency exists to give a pair concentrated time to d
 evelop a book together and to raise the profile of artist book culture and
  of collaboration at the centre of artistic process. WHO APPLIES: a pair m
 ade up of an author with a project for a dialogue book and a visual artist
  collaborating on it. The two do not need to have worked together before\,
  but both must be present during the shared periods. WHAT IS PROVIDED: EUR
  2\,000 gross per month per artist\, up to two return trips per person wit
 hin France and Europe\, and accommodation in the writer's house. STRUCTURE
 : two months in total\, taken as eight weeks which may be split across the
  year from January 2027\, with 70 percent of the time given to creation an
 d 30 percent to mediation and public engagement activities. A videoconfere
 nce is held with the selected laureates beforehand to arrange on-site sche
 duling and the mediation sessions. HOW TO APPLY: follow the official call 
 document downloadable from the residency page\, submitting a single PDF to
  accueil@archipel-butor.fr containing a joint letter\, biographies of both
  practitioners\, the artistic proposal\, technical specifications and the 
 proposed mediation activities. NOTE: the call and application file are in 
 French. Deadline 30 August 2026.\n\nOrganisation: Archipel Butor\nLocation
 : Lucinges (74380)\, Haute-Savoie\, France\, in the house of the writer Mi
 chel Butor.\nAward: Creation grant of EUR 2\,000 gross per month per artis
 t\, so roughly EUR 4\,000 each across the two months and EUR 8\,000 for th
 e pair. Up to two return journeys per person are covered within France and
  Europe. Accommodation is provided in Michel Butor's writer's house. No ap
 plication fee.\nDuration: Two months in total\, taken as eight weeks that 
 may be split\, starting from January 2027. Time is split 70 percent creati
 on and 30 percent mediation activities.\n\nMore: https://www.archipel-buto
 r.fr/residence-creation/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=
 referral
URL:https://www.archipel-butor.fr/residence-creation/?utm_source=artificial
 nouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Lucinges (74380)\, Haute-Savoie\, France\, in the house of the wri
 ter Michel Butor.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:les-antipodes-numeric-2026-the-word@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260905T080002Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260908
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260909
SUMMARY:Deadline: Numeric 2026: The Word (Les antipodes Research and Creati
 on Residency)
DESCRIPTION:Two research and creation residency grants in Agullana\, Catalo
 nia\, for artists\, thinkers and scientists of any branch (formal\, natura
 l\, social\, applied) working at the intersection of art\, culture\, scien
 ce and thought. THIS YEAR'S THEME\, The Word\, is squarely about large lan
 guage models. The framework moves from the word as creative principle acro
 ss traditions (the Judeo-Christian creating God\, the Quran as uncreated w
 ord\, the Sanskrit Om and the Vedas\, mantras that summon rather than desc
 ribe) to science relocating that intuition in the cell (Sagan's four-lette
 r alphabet of DNA as a material text\, Margulis countering that the langua
 ge of life is chemistry\, Witzany holding that all cell interactions are s
 ign-mediated)\, and then to LLMs\, which for the first time detach the wor
 d from the flesh and generate text through a probabilistic architecture tr
 ained on human language. The call asks what LLMs reveal about authorship\,
  originality and meaning when a system generates text that moves\, persuad
 es or deceives without experience or intention\, and whether we can still 
 say the models operate exclusively on human experience once they ingest th
 eir own output. It explicitly wants work that blurs comprehension and simu
 lation\, authorship and recombination\, and it names antecedents worth kno
 wing before applying: Ramon Llull's Ars Magna\, Joan Brossa's Sextina cibe
 rnetica\, Oulipo constraint writing\, oracles and divination\, oral-formul
 aic transmission\, translation as probabilistic approximation\, exquisite 
 corpses\, Burroughs's cut-ups\, automatic writing\, musical improvisation 
 and scientific simulation. Les antipodes asks for engagement 'beyond the i
 nstrumental use of the tools' and away from utopian and dystopian framings
 . WHAT YOU GET: spacious\, luminous\, independent work spaces plus a share
 d terrace in the centre of the village\, spaces for meeting\, dialogue and
  public presentation\, introductions to local\, academic\, artistic and pr
 ofessional contacts on request\, and support organising any activities tha
 t arise. The residency ends with an informal presentation\, and results ma
 y feed into the publications or public programme of Les antipodes. IMPORTA
 NTLY\, this is not an acquisition prize and involves no transfer of rights
 : authorship and intellectual property stay with the creators\, and select
 ion implies no acquisition of the work. Credits on resulting work must ack
 nowledge Numeric 2026 and its collaborators. WHO CAN APPLY: open to everyo
 ne of legal age\, any discipline or origin\, individually or as a collecti
 ve\, with the stipend shared among collective members. Applications must b
 e in Catalan or English whatever the working language of the project\, sub
 mitted through the online form with an optional PDF of up to 5 pages and 1
 0 MB. One application per person or collective. EVALUATION\, in order of w
 eight: singularity and innovation\; relevance to this year's LLM framework
 \; and interaction and transfer of value\, meaning relations with the soci
 al and geographical environment of Agullana and the Emporda and a plan for
  presenting the outcome in editorial\, on-site\, performative\, sound\, au
 diovisual or other form. The call may be declared void if no proposal reac
 hes the required level. PRACTICAL: residents must hold their own health co
 ver (EHIC for EU citizens\, or valid travel or health insurance for Spain 
 otherwise) and are responsible for their own equipment. Supported by the G
 eneralitat de Catalunya\, Agullana and Figueres town councils\, and Accion
  Cultural Espanola through the PICE national residencies programme. Terms 
 are published in Arabic\, Catalan\, English\, Spanish\, French and Chinese
 .\n\nOrganisation: Les antipodes\, with Bolit Centre d'Art Contemporani Gi
 rona and Museu de l'Emporda Figueres\nLocation: Agullana\, Alt Emporda\, C
 atalonia\, Spain. A village of 900 people\, 20 km from Figueres and the Vi
 lafant high-speed rail station\, 40 km from Perpignan\, 60 km from Girona\
 , 160 km from Barcelona.\nAward: Two grants. Each provides a stipend of EU
 R 1\,000 as taxable base (taxes and withholdings apply)\, round-trip trave
 l within the European Union\, and accommodation and meals for the four wee
 ks in Agullana. Travel and meals cover the resident only\, though the shar
 ed apartment can host immediate family by arrangement. Numeric also provid
 es the resources specified in the application and agreed during preparatio
 n. No application fee. Note that if a selected candidate lacks the profess
 ional registration needed to receive the stipend and payment has to be arr
 anged through Les antipodes\, the resulting costs are deducted from the st
 ipend.\nDuration: Four weeks\, 2 to 30 November 2026\, with both residenci
 es running simultaneously. Preceded by one month of preparation and follow
 ed by a two-month closing period. Applications 23 July to 8 September 2026
 \; decision announced 22 September 2026.\n\nMore: https://lesantipodes.com
 /numeric/2026_bases_en.html?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medi
 um=referral
URL:https://lesantipodes.com/numeric/2026_bases_en.html?utm_source=artifici
 alnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Agullana\, Alt Emporda\, Catalonia\, Spain. A village of 900 peopl
 e\, 20 km from Figueres and the Vilafant high-speed rail station\, 40 km f
 rom Perpignan\, 60 km from Girona\, 160 km from Barcelona.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:le-garage-moderne-oasis-residencies-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260905T080002Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260901
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260902
SUMMARY:Deadline: Le Garage Moderne: OASIS International Artist Residencies
  2026 (Bordeaux)
DESCRIPTION:Second year of Le Garage Moderne's residency cycle within the E
 uropean OASIS project\, offering five grants to international artists for 
 two-week stays in Bordeaux. THE SITE IS THE POINT. Le Garage Moderne is a 
 working non-profit car and bicycle workshop with a neighbourhood canteen a
 nd a versatile venue for performing arts\, concerts and community gatherin
 gs\, all at once a productive space\, a public space and a community-drive
 n common. Having just moved back into its 19th-century building after a lo
 ng renovation\, the team describes machines and workbenches back in place 
 and a brand-new playground\, and invites residents to take over the space 
 and explore its physical and social possibilities. They ask for SITE-SPECI
 FIC PROPOSALS that play with the verticality\, the scale of the space\, th
 e community and what they call the hive-like vibe of the venue\, where eve
 ryone runs around\, shouts\, gets their hands dirty and learns in the proc
 ess. DISCIPLINES: any\, explicitly including design\, visual art\, perform
 ing arts\, film\, small-scale architecture\, emergency architecture\, craf
 t-making\, street furniture\, sound\, and interdisciplinary or community-b
 ased practices. Proposals combining artistic creation with the spatial and
  the social are especially encouraged\, and the organisers say plainly tha
 t they want to be surprised. WHAT IS EXPECTED: openness to exchange and co
 llaboration with the 11 permanent residents and with 3 local artists recru
 ited through a separate OASIS call\; participation in 2 days of masterclas
 s open to members and community\, where you describe and share your practi
 ce\, technique and working context with fellow OASIS residents\, local art
 ists\, members and staff\; and a public outcome\, which need not be a larg
 e event but must be something the public can experience. RIGHTS: work prod
 uced during the residency remains the artist's property\, but documentatio
 n of it is made by Le Garage Moderne and will be publicised by them for th
 ree years after the residency. Note this is a different OASIS partner call
  from the OASis In the Park (Naples) and Disappearing Landscape (High Tatr
 as) rounds under the same European project.\n\nOrganisation: Le Garage Mod
 erne (European OASIS project)\nLocation: Bacalan district\, Bordeaux\, Fra
 nce. Le Garage Moderne is a non-profit car garage and bicycle workshop\, a
  canteen serving sponsored meals\, and a cultural venue housing 11 permane
 nt artists\, makers and craftswomen\, recently returned to its 19th-centur
 y building after a two-and-a-half-year renovation.\nAward: Five grants. Ea
 ch artist receives a fee of EUR 1\,300 gross (taxes may apply depending on
  legal status)\, up to EUR 600 for production expenses such as raw materia
 ls\, technical services or special tools\, a lump-sum travel grant scaled 
 to distance with top-ups for green travel options\, a per diem of EUR 45 p
 er day\, and accommodation in a self-catering hotel. Access to the shared 
 tools and spaces of Le Garage Moderne is included\, subject to the associa
 tion's regulations. No application fee.\nDuration: Two weeks on site. Five
  overlapping two-week slots\, and applicants may choose one or more: 9 to 
 22 November\, 16 to 29 November\, 23 November to 6 December\, 30 November 
 to 13 December\, and 7 to 20 December 2026. Deadline 1 September 2026\; pr
 e-selection interviews 15 September\; final selection announced 1 October 
 2026.\n\nMore: https://legaragemoderne.org/oasis2026?utm_source=artificial
 nouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://legaragemoderne.org/oasis2026?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrant
 desk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Bacalan district\, Bordeaux\, France. Le Garage Moderne is a non-p
 rofit car garage and bicycle workshop\, a canteen serving sponsored meals\
 , and a cultural venue housing 11 permanent artists\, makers and craftswom
 en\, recently returned to its 19th-century building after a two-and-a-half
 -year renovation.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:unidee-cittadellarte-visiting-research-curator-2027-28@artificialnouvea
 u.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260905T080002Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260913
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260914
SUMMARY:Deadline: UNIDEE Residency Programs: Visiting Research Curator 2027
 -28
DESCRIPTION:Cittadellarte is recruiting a Visiting Research Curator to shap
 e the research arc of the UNIDEE Residency Modules for 2027-2028\, the rol
 e most recently held by Nina Fiocco and Gaia Martino. Founded by Michelang
 elo Pistoletto in 1999\, the residency has hosted thousands of artists and
  cultural professionals from 52 countries. WHAT THEY WANT: rather than a f
 ixed theme set in advance\, they want a curator who brings their own line 
 of inquiry and develops it collectively through the modules. Suggested the
 matic areas are the evolving landscape of socially engaged art practices\,
  research-driven and process-oriented practice\, and the conditions of cul
 tural work and cultural workers\, aligned with UNIDEE's focus on artistic 
 research in response to ecological\, geopolitical and technological challe
 nges. They also flag a specific interest in how contemporary art practices
  are archived\, drawing on the UNIDEE archive\, which documents over two d
 ecades of socially engaged art initiatives. THIS IS A JOB\, NOT A GRANT: t
 he duties list is long and operational. It covers proposing the overarchin
 g curatorial theme\, designing and delivering a minimum of 8 weekly reside
 ntial modules per year\, writing the curatorial statement and resident ope
 n call\, recruiting mentors and guests\, selecting residents\, hosting and
  facilitating every module in person\, communications and website texts\, 
 a dissemination strategy\, and conceiving and editing publications. There 
 is also teaching-adjacent work with the Unidee Academy of Contemporary Art
 s\, including public lectures and studio visits. Budget the time before ap
 plying. PROFILE: experience curating art in the public sphere and socially
  engaged art\, specialised knowledge of international contemporary art\, e
 xperience designing experimental and collaborative educational activities\
 , and experience with artistic research and dissemination standards. Excel
 lent English required\; Italian welcome but not essential. APPLYING: onlin
 e form with a project proposal of maximum 5000 characters stating your vis
 ion for the modules programme and a development strategy including possibl
 e mentors and guests\, plus a CV\, a portfolio of maximum three relevant p
 rojects\, and TWO LETTERS OF RECOMMENDATION\, so line up your referees ear
 ly. A jury of the UNIDEE Advisory Board\, the Director of Cittadellarte an
 d invited experts decides. They do not give feedback on unsuccessful propo
 sals.\n\nOrganisation: Fondazione Pistoletto Cittadellarte and UNIDEE Resi
 dency Programs\nLocation: Cittadellarte\, Biella\, Italy. Requires in-pers
 on presence at Cittadellarte for a minimum of one month plus the full leng
 th of each module.\nAward: Compensation and contractual terms commensurate
  with the candidate's level of professional experience\, starting from a g
 ross amount of EUR 22\,000 for the appointment\, excluding travel and hosp
 itality\, which are covered separately. No application fee.\nDuration: Tem
 porary appointment from November 2026 to December 2028\, covering the 2027
 -2028 research arc and a minimum of 8 residency modules per year. Deadline
  13 September 2026 at 23:59 CEST\; shortlisted candidates interviewed onli
 ne by mid-October 2026\, with the selected candidate announced a few days 
 later.\n\nMore: https://unidee.cittadellarte.it/activity.html?id=269&utm_s
 ource=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://unidee.cittadellarte.it/activity.html?id=269&utm_source=artific
 ialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Cittadellarte\, Biella\, Italy. Requires in-person presence at Cit
 tadellarte for a minimum of one month plus the full length of each module.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:futures-of-listening-earth-immersion-trembling-planet-2027@artificialno
 uveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260905T080002Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260913
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260914
SUMMARY:Deadline: Futures of Listening x KNMI: Earth Immersion\, Trembling 
 Planet Residency
DESCRIPTION:One residency for an artist\, designer or maker working with so
 und\, exploring spatial sound in relation to seismology\, climate and disc
 harge-related acoustics\, run with the Royal Netherlands Meteorological In
 stitute. THE PREMISE: climate change is making major landslides\, ice-brea
 koff events from marine-terminating glaciers\, and sound-producing ice mov
 ements more common. These generate infrasonic vibrations inaudible to huma
 ns that sometimes spread across the entire planet and into the atmosphere.
  The residency asks how immersive sound techniques can make these events t
 angible and build meaningful sonic narratives from them. WHAT YOU GET ACCE
 SS TO: scientific data on seismological events\, vibrations\, infrasound a
 nd the movement of land and ice masses\, with scientific expertise from KN
 MI (lead researcher Jelle Assink) and artistic guidance from the Futures o
 f Listening team\, relating local events to global impact. RESEARCH QUESTI
 ONS to position your proposal against include how immersive sound can cult
 ivate situated listening to ecological processes and more-than-human encou
 nters\, how artistic and scientific methods of working with environmental 
 data can inform each other\, and what is gained\, lost or ethically at sta
 ke when environmental sounds are translated\, interpreted or reconstructed
  through spatial and synthetic means. DELIVERABLE: residents are expected 
 to create a final spatial sound production using the 4DSOUND system. Other
  outcomes may include immersive installations\, data-driven sonic works\, 
 audiovisual pieces\, software prototypes or reusable methodologies. Experi
 ence in art-science environments is welcome but not required\; you may ins
 tead explain why working in one would benefit your practice. HARD ELIGIBIL
 ITY GATE: applicants must be based in the Netherlands AND registered as an
  artist with the Dutch Chamber of Commerce (KvK). This is not negotiable a
 nd rules out non-NL applicants. Futures of Listening is a Netherlands-base
 d platform for spatial sound\, immersive experience\, performance and inst
 allation art\, founded by FIBER Festival\, 4DSOUND\, Robin Koek and Art of
  Sound at the Royal Conservatoire The Hague\, supported by CIIIC and Stimu
 leringsfonds.\n\nOrganisation: Futures of Listening (FIBER\, 4DSOUND\, Rob
 in Koek\, Art of Sound) in collaboration with KNMI\, the Royal Netherlands
  Meteorological Institute\nLocation: Partially self-hosted\, partially at 
 the 4DSOUND studio in Amsterdam\, and partially at KNMI in De Bilt near Ut
 recht\, Netherlands.\nAward: Total residency fee of EUR 7\,125\, calculate
 d as 25 commissioned days at a day rate of EUR 285. Six of those 25 days c
 an be spent at the 4DSOUND studio working with their team. Some additional
  financial support is available if needed\, including material and travel 
 costs\, but these must be clearly defined and agreed before the residency 
 starts. No application fee.\nDuration: January to March 2027\, structured 
 as 25 commissioned days. Deadline 13 September 2026.\n\nMore: https://fibe
 r.homerun.co/artist-residency-earth-immersion/en?utm_source=artificialnouv
 eaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://fiber.homerun.co/artist-residency-earth-immersion/en?utm_source
 =artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Partially self-hosted\, partially at the 4DSOUND studio in Amsterd
 am\, and partially at KNMI in De Bilt near Utrecht\, Netherlands.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:futures-of-listening-architecture-on-sound-2027@artificialnouveau.githu
 b.io
DTSTAMP:20260905T080002Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260913
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260914
SUMMARY:Deadline: Futures of Listening: Architecture on Sound Residency
DESCRIPTION:One residency for an individual practitioner\, a duo or a studi
 o working at the intersection of sound and space\, exploring architecture 
 through spatial audio. THE PREMISE: architecture is traditionally conceive
 d through form\, structure\, material and light\, with sound treated as so
 mething to be controlled or optimised afterwards. This residency asks what
  happens if architecture begins with sound\, shifting the question from ho
 w a space should sound to how sound can generate space. Spatial audio\, vi
 rtual acoustics and immersive media now allow prototyping of responsive en
 vironments\, impossible architectures and sonic spaces beyond physical con
 straints. RESEARCH QUESTIONS include how sound can become a first principl
 e in architectural and spatial design\, how the acoustic properties of spa
 ce inform the creation and perception of spatial sound\, and how artistic 
 research can shape the future tools\, interfaces and methods of spatial so
 und creation. NOT JUST ARTWORKS: they explicitly say they want new methods
 \, insights and creative infrastructures too. Outcomes may include install
 ations\, spatial audio compositions\, performances\, audiovisual works\, s
 oftware prototypes\, reusable methodologies or modular interfaces. Residen
 ts may collaborate with a 4DSOUND software developer to prototype new tool
 s\, and practitioners who already build their own software\, hardware or c
 reative technologies are encouraged to advance those as part of the reside
 ncy. WHO: architects are the named invitees\, with scenographers\, spatial
  designers\, installation artists\, sound artists\, composers and creative
  technologists also encouraged. Creative coding experience is welcome but 
 not required. Lead researcher is Poul Holleman\, co-founder and director o
 f 4DSOUND. HARD ELIGIBILITY GATE: applicants must be based in the Netherla
 nds AND registered as an artist with the Dutch Chamber of Commerce (KvK). 
 This is not negotiable and rules out non-NL applicants. Futures of Listeni
 ng is a Netherlands-based platform for spatial sound\, immersive experienc
 e\, performance and installation art\, founded by FIBER Festival\, 4DSOUND
 \, Robin Koek and Art of Sound at the Royal Conservatoire The Hague\, supp
 orted by CIIIC and Stimuleringsfonds.\n\nOrganisation: Futures of Listenin
 g (FIBER\, 4DSOUND\, Robin Koek\, Art of Sound)\nLocation: Amsterdam\, Net
 herlands\, with work at the 4DSOUND studio.\nAward: Total residency fee of
  EUR 7\,125\, calculated as 25 commissioned days at a day rate of EUR 285.
  Six of those 25 days can be spent at the 4DSOUND studio working with thei
 r team. Some additional financial support is available if needed\, includi
 ng material and travel costs\, but these must be clearly defined and agree
 d before the residency starts. No application fee.\nDuration: December 202
 6 to February 2027\, structured as 25 commissioned days. Deadline 13 Septe
 mber 2026.\n\nMore: https://fiber.homerun.co/artist-residency-architecture
 -on-sound/en?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://fiber.homerun.co/artist-residency-architecture-on-sound/en?utm_
 source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Amsterdam\, Netherlands\, with work at the 4DSOUND studio.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:futures-of-listening-worlding-narratives-roaming-sound-space-2027@artif
 icialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260905T080002Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260913
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260914
SUMMARY:Deadline: Futures of Listening: Worlding Narratives\, Roaming Sound
  Space Residency
DESCRIPTION:One residency to research and develop an interactive listening 
 experience built on non-linear sound narratives\, experienced by navigatin
 g through a series of virtual spaces or layers. NO VISUALS: Worlding Narra
 tives centres sound as the primary medium for immersion and intentionally 
 removes dominant visual layers such as VR\, AR\, projection or video. THE 
 DESIGN PROBLEM they want tackled is translation and adaptation\, specifica
 lly the shift from one visitor in a headphone-mediated experience to rende
 ring for a multi-speaker system for group interaction. That change in dyna
 mics\, reproduction and perspective is integral to the residency scope. RE
 SEARCH QUESTIONS include how immersive sound can function as a primary nar
 rative medium across speculative and non-fictional realities\, what new na
 rrative structures and dramaturgical methods become possible when the crea
 tive process originates from sound rather than adapting frameworks from ot
 her media\, and how interactive sonic structures can create navigable list
 ening experiences designed for and by multiple intelligences. Outcomes may
  include interactive sonic prototypes\, spatial audio compositions\, immer
 sive listening experiences\, narrative sound works or software prototypes.
  WORTH NOTING: your residency runs in tandem with resident Pinar Temiz\, a
 n expert in sound design for gaming environments\, with the opportunity to
  work together. Lead researcher is Dr Angela McArthur of UCL\, with resear
 ch support from Dr meLe yamomo of the University of Amsterdam. WHO: media 
 and sound artists\, sound designers\, musicians producing their own sonic 
 work\, field recordists\, writers\, composers and game sound designers. Ex
 perience with interactive audio\, spatial sound tools or game-sound workfl
 ows is welcome but not required. HARD ELIGIBILITY GATE: applicants must be
  based in the Netherlands AND registered as an artist with the Dutch Chamb
 er of Commerce (KvK). This is not negotiable and rules out non-NL applican
 ts. Futures of Listening is a Netherlands-based platform for spatial sound
 \, immersive experience\, performance and installation art\, founded by FI
 BER Festival\, 4DSOUND\, Robin Koek and Art of Sound at the Royal Conserva
 toire The Hague\, supported by CIIIC and Stimuleringsfonds.\n\nOrganisatio
 n: Futures of Listening (FIBER\, 4DSOUND\, Robin Koek\, Art of Sound)\nLoc
 ation: Amsterdam\, Netherlands\, with remote and studio access to the 4DSO
 UND infrastructure.\nAward: Total residency fee of EUR 7\,125\, calculated
  as 25 commissioned days at a day rate of EUR 285. Six of those 25 days ca
 n be spent at the 4DSOUND studio working with their team. Some additional 
 financial support is available if needed\, including material and travel c
 osts\, but these must be clearly defined and agreed before the residency s
 tarts. No application fee.\nDuration: January to March 2027\, structured a
 s 25 commissioned days\, running in tandem with fellow Worlding Narratives
  resident Pinar Temiz. Deadline 13 September 2026.\n\nMore: https://fiber.
 homerun.co/artist-residency-worlding-narratives/en?utm_source=artificialno
 uveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://fiber.homerun.co/artist-residency-worlding-narratives/en?utm_so
 urce=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Amsterdam\, Netherlands\, with remote and studio access to the 4DS
 OUND infrastructure.
END:VEVENT
